-- Breaking BCS: 2차 기사 50건 (영문) — 바로 발행 (대표님 요청), Opinion 은 작성 중(초안)
-- Supabase SQL 에디터에서 실행 (breakingbcs-2026-10.sql 다음). 여러 번 실행해도 된다 (같은 제목 기사는 건너뛴다)
--   · Bryan·College Station 시·카운티·학교구, Texas A&M 공식 발표, 미국 연방·텍사스 주 기관 자료를 바탕으로 AI가 새로 쓴 기사. 본문 끝에 AI 활용 표시
--   · 기자명: 편집장 John Lee. 본문 첫머리는 미국 신문식 데이트라인(예: COLLEGE STATION —)
--     John Lee 계정이 있으면 작성자도 그 계정 (없으면 이 매체 편집장 → 관리자 → 총관리자 계정. 기자명은 어느 경우나 John Lee)
--   · 사진: 저장소 /sites/breakingbcs/photos/ (위키미디어 공용 공개 라이선스 자료사진, 배포가 끝난 뒤에 보인다)
--   · 기사마다 확인할 점·원문 주소·이용 조건을 AI 메모(편집 메모)에 남겼다

with ctx as (
  select o.id as outlet_id,
         coalesce(
           (select p.id from profiles p where p.full_name = 'John Lee' order by (p.outlet_id = o.id) desc nulls last, p.created_at limit 1),
           (select p.id from profiles p where p.outlet_id = o.id and p.role = 'editor' order by p.created_at limit 1),
           (select p.id from profiles p where p.outlet_id = o.id and p.role = 'admin' order by p.created_at limit 1),
           (select p.id from profiles p where (to_jsonb(p) ->> 'is_super') = 'true' order by p.created_at limit 1)
         ) as author_id
  from outlets o where o.name = 'Breaking BCS'
  order by o.created_at limit 1
)
insert into articles (outlet_id, category_id, author_id, title, body, excerpt, thumbnail_url, status, published_at, tags, byline, ai_notes)
select ctx.outlet_id, c.id, ctx.author_id, v.title, v.body, v.excerpt, v.thumb, v.status::article_status, v.published_at, v.tags, 'John Lee', v.notes
from ctx
cross join (values
  ('baseball', 'published', 'Former Aggie Outfielder Tyler Naquin Enters Texas A&M Athletics Hall of Fame', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Former Texas A&amp;M outfielder Tyler Naquin was inducted into the Texas A&amp;M University Athletic Hall of Fame on Friday night, part of an eight-member class honored by the Texas A&amp;M Lettermen''s Association at the 48th Burgess Banquet in the Ford Hall of Champions.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-sp-08.webp" alt="Tyler Naquin during a Cleveland Indians game against the Kansas City Royals in his rookie season (file photo, 2016). Photo: Erik Drost / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 2.0)"><p><em>▲ Tyler Naquin during a Cleveland Indians game against the Kansas City Royals in his rookie season (file photo, 2016). Photo: Erik Drost / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 2.0)</em></p><p>Naquin, from Spring, lettered from 2010 to 2012 and batted .346 in his Aggie career with 112 RBIs and 153 runs scored in 187 games, 176 of them starts, according to Texas A&amp;M Athletics. The Aggies reached the NCAA Tournament in each of his three seasons and won Big 12 Tournament titles in 2010 and 2011.</p><p>His 2011 season stands out. Naquin started all 68 games as Texas A&amp;M reached the College World Series and led the team in batting average (.381), hits (104), runs (68), doubles (23), triples (7) and walks (29), the athletics department said. He put together a 27-game hitting streak, the second-longest in program history, and hit for the cycle with six RBIs against Houston on March 6 of that year.</p><p>Naquin was named Big 12 Player of the Year in 2011, twice won the team''s Marion Pugh Most Valuable Player Award and is one of 10 two-time All-Americans in Texas A&amp;M baseball history. His career totals of 238 hits, 49 doubles and 13 triples each rank among the program''s top 15, the school said.</p><p>Cleveland drafted Naquin 15th overall in 2012. He debuted in the majors on April 5, 2016, becoming the 52nd Aggie to reach the big leagues, and finished third in American League Rookie of the Year voting that season after hitting .296 with 14 home runs for a team that reached the World Series. Over eight major league seasons with Cleveland, Cincinnati, the New York Mets and the Chicago White Sox, he hit .263 with 61 home runs and 237 RBIs.</p><p>The rest of the Class of 2026 is softball shortstop Carrie Austgen, football''s Myles Garrett, swimmer Sarah Henry, cross country runner Henry Lelei, javelin thrower Maggie Malone-Hardin, diver Jaele Patrick and decathlete Lindon Victor. The late Terry Price, a former Aggie defensive lineman and assistant coach, received Hall of Honor recognition, and former women''s basketball player Kelly Krauskopf received the Lifetime Achievement Award.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Tyler Naquin, a two-time All-American who hit .381 for Texas A&M''s 2011 College World Series team, was inducted into the Texas A&M Athletics Hall of Fame on Friday with seven other Aggie greats.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-sp-08.webp', '2026-09-12T12:30:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Texas A&M baseball','Tyler Naquin','Hall of Fame','Aggie history','MLB']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Release says Naquin finished third in Rookie of the Year voting; ''American League'' added because Cleveland is an AL team — confirm or remove.
• 확인할 점: ''Friday night'' = Sept. 11, 2026 (release posted Sept. 12 says ''Friday evening'').
• 원문(Texas A&M Athletics — Eight Aggie Legends Inducted into Texas A&M Athletics Hall of Fame (Sept. 12, 2026)): https://12thman.com/news/2026/09/12/eight-aggie-legends-inducted-into-texas-athletic-hall-of-fame
• 이용 조건: Text: facts from Texas A&M Athletics (12thman.com) releases, rewritten; quotes as published by the athletics department / Photo: CC BY 2.0, Erik Drost, File:Tyler Naquin on June 4, 2016.jpg
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('business', 'published', 'College Station Weighs Housing, Roads and Labor Effects of Grimes County Terafab', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — College Station officials told the City Council on Sept. 10 that the Terafab semiconductor project in neighboring Grimes County will bring growth and service demands to the city even though College Station will not directly receive tax revenue from the plant.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-bz-06.webp" alt="Close-up of a silicon wafer patterned with computer chips (file photo, 2020). It is not from the Terafab project. Photo: Laura Ockel / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ Close-up of a silicon wafer patterned with computer chips (file photo, 2020). It is not from the Terafab project. Photo: Laura Ockel / Unsplash</em></p><p>Gov. Greg Abbott announced Aug. 6 that SpaceX will build the plant, a vertically integrated chip factory whose first phase carries a capital investment of more than $16.8 billion and is expected to create 3,000 jobs. The state extended a $30 million Texas Enterprise Fund grant, and the project qualified under the state''s JETI incentive program, according to the governor''s office.</p><p>In his presentation, Chief Development Officer Michael Ostrowski said project agreements call for 1,500 to 3,000 direct jobs, growing to more than 6,000 jobs counting indirect and induced work, along with roughly 5,000 construction workers a year over a buildout of 10 to 11 years, according to a city summary published Sept. 15.</p><p>The city projects that the 77845 ZIP code in South College Station will absorb the largest share of new residents tied to the plant''s jobs. Officials pointed to about 7,000 acres of developable land east of the city limits and a housing pipeline of nearly 11,000 planned units, enough for about 28,000 residents.</p><p>William D. Fitch Parkway runs at about 40% of capacity and would reach about 60% under a 3,000-job scenario, the presentation said. Utility service east of the city is split among Bryan Texas Utilities and the Wellborn and Wickson Creek special utility districts.</p><p>The city listed early lodging shortages from construction crews, higher construction costs for public projects and wage competition for skilled workers among the challenges. It cited the effects of SpaceX''s Starbase near Brownsville and Samsung''s chip plant in Taylor as examples.</p><p>Council members discussed construction timelines, long-term housing demand and the need for regional interlocal agreements, the summary said.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'A College Station presentation says SpaceX''s Terafab chip plant in Grimes County could bring 1,500 to 3,000 direct jobs, with South College Station absorbing much of the new resident growth.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-bz-06.webp', '2026-09-16T10:10:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Terafab','Grimes County','SpaceX','College Station','growth']::text[], '• 확인할 점: The city''s live blog says ''$16.8 billion in Phase I capital''; the Sept. 15 summary says ''$17 billion''. We used the governor''s ''more than $16.8 billion''.
• 확인할 점: Job figures vary by source (governor: 3,000 new jobs in phase one; city: 1,500-3,000 direct). Both attributed.
• 확인할 점: The city blog said College Station ''will not directly receive tax revenue from the Terafab tax base''; we wrote property tax — editor may prefer ''tax revenue from the plant''s tax base''.
• 확인할 점: Based on city summary of the presentation, not the slide deck itself.
• 원문(City of College Station blog — ICYMI Video: The Terafab project''s local impact presented to the City Council (Sept. 15, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/09/15/icymi-video-the-terafab-projects-local-impact-presented-to-the-city-council-sept-10/
• 원문(City of College Station blog — Live from City Hall (Sept. 10, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/09/10/live-from-city-hall-thursdays-city-council-meeting-sept-10/
• 원문(Office of the Texas Governor — Governor Abbott Announces SpaceX Expansion In Grimes County (Aug. 6, 2026)): https://gov.texas.gov/news/post/governor-abbott-announces-spacex-expansion-in-grimes-county
• 이용 조건: Text: facts rewritten from public government sources; no text copied / Photo: Unsplash License, Laura Ockel / Unsplash, https://unsplash.com/photos/silicon-wafer-with-iridescent-microchip-grid-qOx9KsvpqcM
