  ('business', 'published', 'Bryan Extends Legends Event Center Management Deal Through 2032', '<p>BRYAN — The Bryan City Council has extended its contract with the company that runs Legends Event Center through 2032 and added the neighboring Travis Fields baseball and softball complex to the same contract, the city said Sept. 16.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-bz-07.webp" alt="Aerial view of four baseball and softball fields in Chillicothe, Ohio (file photo). It is not Travis Fields or Legends Event Center. Photo: Brevan Combs / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ Aerial view of four baseball and softball fields in Chillicothe, Ohio (file photo). It is not Travis Fields or Legends Event Center. Photo: Brevan Combs / Unsplash</em></p><p>At a Sept. 15 special meeting, the council approved an amendment to its management agreement with Sports Facilities Management LLC, which does business as The Sports Facilities Companies, exercising the city''s option to extend the deal. The Travis Fields agreement was assigned to the company from Rally Cap Investments LLC.</p><p>Both venues are in Travis Bryan Midtown Park, a 150-acre former municipal golf course and lake that the city has turned into a sports and recreation complex since 2020.</p><p>Legends, a multipurpose sports and events facility of more than 122,000 square feet, has drawn more than 930,000 visitors since it opened in December 2022, the city said. Its tourism-based events have produced a total economic impact of more than $108 million over four years, including an expected $40 million this fiscal year, according to the city.</p><p>Travis Fields, which opened in February 2022 with three synthetic-turf fields and eight covered batting cages, recorded more than 68,000 visitors and $14 million in economic impact in fiscal 2025, the city said.</p><p>"It''s impossible to overstate how vital sports tourism is for our community," Mayor Bobby Gutierrez said in the release, adding that the facilities bring business to local hotels, restaurants and stores.</p><p>John Friebele, executive director of Destination Bryan, the city''s tourism office, said in the release that events at Legends "generate more money for our hotels, restaurants and gas stations."</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Bryan extended its Legends Event Center management agreement through 2032 and added Travis Fields. The city says Legends'' tourism events have produced more than $108 million in economic impact.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-bz-07.webp', '2026-09-17T09:10:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Bryan','sports tourism','Legends Event Center','Travis Fields','economic impact']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Economic impact figures are the city''s/operator''s estimates; methodology not given.
• 확인할 점: Contract financial terms (management fee) not stated in the release.
• 확인할 점: Earlier parks story mentioned Travis Major Field renovation; this story is about the management contract and tourism impact, not park upgrades.
• 확인할 점: Photo location (Laramie, WY) taken from file title; confirm with file page description.
• 원문(City of Bryan — City of Bryan Celebrating Future of Legends Event Center & Travis Fields (Sept. 16, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/city-of-bryan-celebrating-future-of-legends-event-center-travis-fields/
• 이용 조건: Text: facts rewritten from public government sources; no text copied / Photo: Unsplash License, Brevan Combs / Unsplash, https://unsplash.com/photos/B_dS1Mv69yI
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('latest', 'published', 'Bryan Police Report 5.8% Drop in Crime Through First Seven Months of 2026', '<p>BRYAN — Reported crime in Bryan fell 5.8% during the first seven months of 2026 compared with the same period last year, the Bryan Police Department reported in figures released by the city.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-04.webp" alt="Police cars with emergency lights on at night in New Haven, Connecticut (file photo). It does not show Bryan police. Photo: Martin Podsiad / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ Police cars with emergency lights on at night in New Haven, Connecticut (file photo). It does not show Bryan police. Photo: Martin Podsiad / Unsplash</em></p><p>Crimes against persons dropped 6%, property crimes fell 9.6% and crimes against society, a category that includes offenses such as drug violations, declined 0.7%, according to the city.</p><p>The city listed declines in several major categories: robbery fell 35.3% to 11 offenses, burglary 22.5% to 62, motor vehicle theft 34% to 35, larceny and theft 5% to 452, and aggravated assault 6.5% to 87. One murder was reported, down from two in the same period of 2025.</p><p>The longer trend is steeper. Bryan recorded 4,889 serious offenses under the FBI''s Uniform Crime Reporting Part I categories in 1991, its all-time high, and 1,286 in 2025, a drop of almost 74%, the city said. Over the same two decades, the city''s population grew from 69,851 in 2005 to 92,664, while the police department grew from 160 employees to 196.</p><p>"Looking at a single month or even a single year of crime data does not always tell the complete story," Police Chief Dean Swartzlander said in the city''s release. "Bryan is a significantly larger community today, but over the long term, reported crime has declined substantially."</p><p>The department said it relies on comparative statistics and intelligence-led policing, using crime reports, calls for service and investigative data to identify trends, repeat offenders and high-risk locations and to direct patrols and investigators there.</p><p>"We are encouraged by the progress, but we are not satisfied by it," Swartzlander said, adding that violent crime "will always receive our full attention."</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Bryan police say reported crime fell 5.8% in the first seven months of 2026 compared with 2025, with robbery, burglary and auto theft down sharply, continuing a decades-long decline.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-04.webp', '2026-09-21T11:30:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Bryan','Bryan Police Department','crime statistics','public safety']::text[], '• 확인할 점: The ''down from two'' murder figure is derived from the release''s ''one offense, down 50%''.
• 확인할 점: ''Crimes against society'' explained with drug violations as an example (standard FBI NIBRS category); the release itself did not give examples.
• 확인할 점: The 1991 vs. 2025 comparison uses UCR Part I counts while the 2026 partial-year figures use persons/property/society categories (NIBRS-style); the two are different measures and are presented separately.
• 확인할 점: Figures are the department''s own preliminary numbers; not independently checked against FBI or Texas DPS data.
• 확인할 점: Photo is a generic light bar, likely not a U.S. vehicle; caption says it is not a Bryan police vehicle.
• 원문(City of Bryan — Crime Rate Falling in Bryan, Supporting High Quality of Life for the Community (Sept. 21, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/crime-rate-falling-in-bryan-supporting-high-quality-of-life-for-the-community/
• 이용 조건: Text: facts from City of Bryan news release, rewritten; quotes from the release / Photo: Unsplash License, Martin Podsiad / Unsplash, https://unsplash.com/photos/police-cars-on-dark-residential-street-wrdtA9lew9E
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('business', 'published', 'Harvey District Survey Finds Wish for Reinvestment, New Ideas for Post Oak Mall', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Residents and business owners who weighed in on College Station''s Harvey District plan want reinvestment in aging and underused properties, including Post Oak Mall, without a complete remake of the area, according to a summary the city released Sept. 22.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-bz-08.webp" alt="An empty two-level shopping mall interior (file photo; location not given). It is not Post Oak Mall. Photo: Hunter Newton / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ An empty two-level shopping mall interior (file photo; location not given). It is not Post Oak Mall. Photo: Hunter Newton / Unsplash</em></p><p>The Harvey District is the commercial and residential area around Harvey Road. The city gathered comments through an open house, an online survey and map, four visioning workshops, pop-up events, comment cards and social media, the summary said.</p><p>Of 265 people who completed the community survey, 52% rated the district as fair and 34% rated it poor or very poor. Aging buildings, dated appearances, vacant properties, large parking lots and the condition of Post Oak Mall were among the most common concerns, according to the city.</p><p>Supporting local businesses drew more priority votes than any other topic at the kickoff open house. In the survey, 59% said they want the district to become a lively mixed-use destination, and 48% chose a more attractive gateway into the city.</p><p>Post Oak Mall came up as a major opportunity in nearly every activity. Ideas ranged from renovating the mall to adding mixed-use development, restaurants, entertainment and event space, cutting back surface parking and improving links to Wolf Pen Creek. The city said there was no agreement on a single future for the mall.</p><p>Participants were split on traffic. Some said medians and turn limits make roads safer, while others said they make businesses hard to reach. Many also asked to protect affordable housing and avoid displacing current residents.</p><p>The city said the input is not a scientific sample or a final decision. Planners will use it with market and technical studies and guidance from the Harvey District Working Group to develop possible future scenarios.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Most respondents to a College Station survey rated the Harvey District fair or poor. Many want reinvestment, a mixed-use destination and new uses for Post Oak Mall while keeping local businesses.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-bz-08.webp', '2026-09-23T14:30:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['College Station','Harvey District','Post Oak Mall','redevelopment','planning']::text[], '• 확인할 점: The summary does not define the district boundaries; ''area around Harvey Road'' is our general description — confirm with city plan page.
• 확인할 점: The input is not a scientific sample (city''s own caveat, included).
• 확인할 점: No timeline was given for the scenario phase.
• 확인할 점: Photo is from 2012; the building may have changed.
• 원문(City of College Station blog — What we heard: Key takeaways from community input about the Harvey District''s future (Sept. 22, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/09/22/what-we-heard-key-takeaways-from-community-input-about-the-harvey-districts-future/
• 이용 조건: Text: facts rewritten from public government sources; no text copied / Photo: Unsplash License, Hunter Newton / Unsplash, https://unsplash.com/photos/an-empty-multi-level-shopping-mall-interior-with-potted-plants-h3AyySNa-Ag
