  ('business', 'published', 'College Station Approves Land Sales, Incentives for Bass Pro Shops Project', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — The College Station City Council on Sept. 24 unanimously approved real estate contracts and economic development agreements that will bring a Bass Pro Shops store and a mixed-use retail project to 28 city-owned acres in the Midtown Business Park, the city said.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-bz-05.webp" alt="A rural two-lane highway the photographer tagged as Texas (file photo, 2024; exact location not given). It is not State Highway 6 or the Midtown project site. Photo: Jon Tyson / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ A rural two-lane highway the photographer tagged as Texas (file photo, 2024; exact location not given). It is not State Highway 6 or the Midtown project site. Photo: Jon Tyson / Unsplash</em></p><p>The site lies along State Highway 6 north of William D. Fitch Parkway, next to Costco and in front of the planned South College Station baseball and softball complex, according to Grow College Station, the city''s economic development office.</p><p>Bass Pro Shops will buy about 14 acres for about $5.8 million to build a 98,000-square-foot Outdoor World store, which is expected to open in 2028. Dallas-based Corinth Properties will buy the remaining 14 acres for about $5.04 million for a project with more than 100,000 square feet of commercial space, restaurants and a central plaza, according to the city''s meeting blog. Final prices depend on land surveys.</p><p>The agreements, approved under Chapter 380 of the Texas Local Government Code, include a $3.5 million operations reimbursement to Bass Pro Shops, payable after construction. The city will also return part of the sales tax the store generates for 10 years, starting at 80% and falling to 35%, for an average of 60%, plus $167,105 in permit and impact fee reimbursements.</p><p>The store is projected to produce $35 million in annual sales, with at least $20 million taxable locally, the city said. The project is also seeking incentives from Brazos County.</p><p>Corinth will receive a reimbursement on its land purchase, with a credit of up to $696,960 at closing, in exchange for building and maintaining the plaza, the city said.</p><p>The 28 acres were part of 339 acres the city bought in 2000 for the Midtown Business Park at $11,250 per acre, putting the original cost of this tract at about $315,000, according to the blog. The agreements include a 180-day inspection period.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'College Station will sell 28 city-owned acres in Midtown for about $10.84 million to Bass Pro Shops and Corinth Properties, with sales tax and other reimbursements for the planned 2028 store.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-bz-05.webp', '2026-09-25T08:30:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['College Station','Bass Pro Shops','economic development','Midtown','incentives']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Mayor John Nichols was absent; vote was unanimous among members present (blog).
• 확인할 점: Sale prices are approximate; final amounts depend on surveys ($9.59/sq ft for Bass Pro, $8/sq ft for Corinth).
• 확인할 점: The release calls the company both ''Corinth Properties'' and ''Corinth Group''; we used Corinth Properties.
• 확인할 점: Brazos County incentive request status not confirmed.
• 확인할 점: Earlier editorial (bcs-bz-05) referenced this deal; this is the first straight news story on the terms.
• 확인할 점: Photo: SH 40 is William D. Fitch Parkway per TxDOT designation; photo is not of the site.
• 원문(Grow College Station — City Council approves agreements with Bass Pro Shops, Corinth Properties (Sept. 24, 2026)): https://grow.cstx.gov/news/posts/city-council-approves-agreements-with-bass-pro-shops-corinth-properties/
