  ('latest', 'published', 'College Station Council Votes 4-2 to Approve Flock License Plate Camera Agreements', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — The College Station City Council voted 4-2 on Thursday night to approve new agreements with Flock Group for automatic license plate reader cameras used by the College Station Police Department.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-06.webp" alt="Security cameras mounted on a pole (file photo). They are not Flock license plate reader cameras and were not photographed in College Station. Photo: Tomas Ryant / Pexels"><p><em>▲ Security cameras mounted on a pole (file photo). They are not Flock license plate reader cameras and were not photographed in College Station. Photo: Tomas Ryant / Pexels</em></p><p>The council approved an agreement of up to $158,000 for cameras on Texas Department of Transportation roads and a $141,062 agreement for cameras on other roads, according to the city''s summary of the Sept. 24 meeting. Council members Mark Smith and Bob Yancy voted no. Mayor John Nichols was absent.</p><p>The solar-powered cameras photograph the backs of passing vehicles day and night and record the make, model and color along with the plate, making the data searchable for criminal investigations, the city said. Police use the system to find stolen vehicles and vehicles tied to shootings, burglaries, robberies, kidnappings and other crimes.</p><p>Police told the council that no search of the system can be run without reasonable suspicion, the same standard needed for a traffic stop. Each search must include the offense type, a case number, the reason, vehicle details, location and time. The department controls which outside agencies may search its network, and every search is kept indefinitely, the city said.</p><p>An audit tool that went live April 14 flags search patterns or plate lookups that fall outside an agency''s normal use or policy, according to the presentation. The department''s written policy says it will use the technology only for official purposes and will respect "the public''s privacy interests."</p><p>Comparing March through September of 2025 and 2026, police reported that home burglaries fell from 63 to 29, car burglaries from 113 to 18 and firearm thefts from 23 to 18. The city said the drop in property crime "correlates with" the cameras'' introduction. Police also listed cases in which the system helped locate a missing child and identify suspects in robbery and shooting investigations.</p><p>The cameras have drawn public objections. At the council''s Oct. 8 meeting, one resident spoke against using Flock cameras and another suggested changes to the department''s license plate reader policies, the city''s meeting summary shows.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'The College Station City Council voted 4-2 to approve nearly $300,000 in agreements for Flock license plate reader cameras after police described search limits, audits and local crime declines.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-06.webp', '2026-09-25T09:20:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['College Station','College Station Police Department','license plate readers','City Council','privacy']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Source is the city''s live blog of the Sept. 24 meeting, which says it is not the official minutes. Contract terms (length, number of cameras) were not stated; not included.
• 확인할 점: The crime comparison and the ''correlates with'' wording are the city''s/police department''s; causation is not established and the story attributes it.
• 확인할 점: The story says the council approved ''new agreements''; the blog does not say whether these renew existing contracts, though the March-September comparison implies cameras were already in use in 2026. Confirm wording (''keep'' in headline) with CSPD if needed.
• 확인할 점: Last paragraph cites the Oct. 8 Hear Visitors segment (after the vote); speakers'' names left out.
• 확인할 점: Bass Pro Shops/Corinth Midtown agreements from the same meeting are business news and left out.
• 확인할 점: Photo location not given on Commons; caption says only that it is not College Station.
• 원문(City of College Station — Live from City Hall: Thursday''s City Council meeting (Sept. 24, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/09/24/live-from-city-hall-thursdays-city-council-meeting-sept-24/
• 원문(City of College Station — Live from City Hall: Thursday''s City Council meeting (Oct. 8, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/10/08/live-from-city-hall-thursdays-city-council-meeting-oct-8/
