  ('latest', 'published', 'College Station Utilities Earns National Cybersecurity Designation', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — College Station Utilities, the city-owned electric provider, has received a new national cybersecurity designation from the American Public Power Association, the city announced.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-08.webp" alt="Power lines at the University of Washington&#x27;s electrical substation in Seattle (file photo, 2024). It does not show College Station Utilities equipment. Photo: Zoshua Colah / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ Power lines at the University of Washington''s electrical substation in Seattle (file photo, 2024). It does not show College Station Utilities equipment. Photo: Zoshua Colah / Unsplash</em></p><p>The association, which represents community-owned electric utilities, awarded its first Cybersecurity Accelerator Program designations to 60 public power utilities across the country, according to the city. The designation lasts two years.</p><p>To qualify, utilities had to show proficiency in internal controls, cybersecurity governance and training, incident response and cyber risk management, the city said. They also had to show they can protect critical systems over time, train employees to spot and handle cyber threats, keep incident response plans current and maintain long-term security practices.</p><p>"We go to work every day to ensure safe and reliable service to this community, and it''s nice to see that hard work recognized nationally," said Glenn Gavit, the utility''s electric director.</p><p>Fred Christie, who chairs the association''s review panel for the program, said in the city''s release that the designees "have distinguished themselves as committed to protecting their utilities and the U.S. electric grid for the benefit of their communities."</p><p>The announcement came during Public Power Week, when the city recognized its electric utility and customer service employees. More than 55 million Americans and 5.1 million Texans get electricity from community-owned utilities, the city said.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'College Station Utilities is one of 60 public power utilities nationwide to receive the American Public Power Association''s first Cybersecurity Accelerator Program designation, the city said.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-08.webp', '2026-10-05T13:25:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['College Station','College Station Utilities','cybersecurity','public power','utilities']::text[], '• 확인할 점: The list of 60 utilities and whether BTU (Bryan) also received the designation was not checked on APPA''s site; not stated.
• 확인할 점: Fred Christie is also CIO of Easton Utilities in Maryland per release; omitted for length.
• 확인할 점: The Public Power Week post on cstx.gov was a short teaser; the full blog was not read. Only the 55 million / 5.1 million figures were used from it.
• 원문(City of College Station — College Station Utilities earns national cybersecurity designation (Oct. 5, 2026)): https://www.cstx.gov/news/posts/college-station-utilities-earns-national-cybersecurity-designation/
• 원문(City of College Station — Public Power Week highlights CSU''s hard-working electric utility workers (Oct. 5, 2026)): https://www.cstx.gov/news/posts/public-power-week-highlights-csus-hard-working-electric-utility-workers/
• 이용 조건: Text: facts from City of College Station releases, rewritten; quotes from the release / Photo: Unsplash License, Zoshua Colah / Unsplash, https://unsplash.com/photos/a-black-and-white-photo-of-power-lines-and-a-bridge-tQN6r0xwprU
