  ('latest', 'published', 'College Station Staff Advise Against Safe-Passing Ordinance for Cyclists', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — College Station city staff told the City Council on Thursday that a proposed safe-passing ordinance meant to protect bicyclists, pedestrians and motorcyclists would be hard to enforce, and recommended not pursuing it for now.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-05.webp" alt="High-rise residential buildings in the Northgate area of College Station (file photo, 2026). Photo: Ggskater / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)"><p><em>▲ High-rise residential buildings in the Northgate area of College Station (file photo, 2026). Photo: Ggskater / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)</em></p><p>The ordinance discussed Oct. 8 would require drivers of passenger vehicles to leave at least 3 feet when passing "vulnerable road users" and commercial vehicles to leave 6 feet, according to the city''s meeting summary. The group would include pedestrians, runners, children, cyclists, motorcyclists, people with disabilities, construction and maintenance workers, tow truck operators, stranded motorists and people on horseback or farm equipment.</p><p>Violations would carry fines of $25 to $500 and would be handled in College Station Municipal Court as criminal citations. But state law would limit the offense to a Class C misdemeanor, and because of the burden of proof, staff recommended against adopting this type of ordinance at this time, the city said.</p><p>The city''s Active Transportation Advisory Board gave mixed feedback when it reviewed the idea in September. It found the ordinance would support safety goals and make the city friendlier to cyclists, but would be difficult to enforce, and it recommended folding the effort into a "Share the Road" education campaign called for in the city''s recently adopted Active Transportation Master Plan.</p><p>The council also heard results of outreach to property owners on a possible overlay district that would limit building heights in Northgate, the entertainment district across from Texas A&amp;M. The area under study is generally bounded by First Street, Lodge Street, University Drive and Church Avenue. The city''s summary did not report a vote on the overlay or on the safe-passing ordinance.</p><p>In another discussion, the council reviewed fees and rules for the College Station Cemetery and the Memorial Cemetery at the Aggie Field of Honor. The Parks and Recreation Advisory Board recommended a 10% fee increase, a ban on reselling plots for profit and limiting purchases to individuals, with no separate rates for nonresidents. Standard plots at the Texas Avenue cemetery have not been available since 2024, the city said.</p><p>On its consent agenda, the council approved a $5.54 million contract with Integrated Power for transmission line construction, maintenance and repair. A proposed contract for new slides at Adamson Lagoon was pulled from the agenda.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'College Station staff recommended against a local safe-passing ordinance for cyclists and pedestrians, citing enforcement hurdles. The council also discussed Northgate building heights and cemetery fees.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-05.webp', '2026-10-09T08:15:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['College Station','City Council','bicycle safety','Northgate','ordinance']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Source is the city''s live blog, which says it is not the official minutes. Whether the council gave formal direction on the safe-passing ordinance, Northgate overlay or cemetery fees was not stated; story says only what staff recommended and that the items were discussed.
• 확인할 점: The live blog''s intro paragraph on cstx.gov mistakenly says ''Sept. 24''; the blog itself is dated and titled Oct. 8.
• 확인할 점: Photo description on Commons names three buildings (Aspire Reserve, Honcho, Rev Northgate); building names may be visible on the towers. Caption does not name them. Editor may want to check the image for prominent signage.
• 확인할 점: Consent agenda also included a $500,000 H&B Construction boring contract, Big River Street parking ban, BVWACS budget and a JAG grant interlocal; omitted for length.
• 원문(City of College Station — Live from City Hall: Thursday''s City Council meeting (Oct. 8, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/10/08/live-from-city-hall-thursdays-city-council-meeting-oct-8/
• 원문(City of College Station — 5 things to watch at Thursday''s City Council meeting (Oct. 7, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/10/07/5-things-to-watch-at-thursdays-city-council-meeting-102/
