  ('texas-am', 'published', 'Texas A&M Wins Sixth State Gold Award for Serving Student Veterans', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Texas A&amp;M University has received a gold-level award from the Texas Veterans Commission for the sixth time, recognizing its support for student veterans and military-connected students.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-cv-10.webp" alt="American flags fly atop Kyle Field on the Texas A&amp;M University campus in College Station (file photo). It is not related to the veterans award. Photo: Iain Cridland / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ American flags fly atop Kyle Field on the Texas A&amp;M University campus in College Station (file photo). It is not related to the veterans award. Photo: Iain Cridland / Unsplash</em></p><p>The university was named a Gold Recipient of the commission''s 2026 Veteran Education Excellence Recognition Award, Texas A&amp;M said in an Oct. 16 release. The state agency created the award in 2021 to recognize Texas colleges and universities for services such as student organizations, academic support, mental health and disability services and housing policies for veterans.</p><p>Texas A&amp;M''s Don and Ellie Knauss Veteran Resource and Support Center serves more than 1,600 veteran and military students on the main College Station campus and at 12 branch campuses and sites, the university said.</p><p>During the 2025-26 academic year, the center administered $685,298 in scholarships, financial aid and childcare assistance, according to the release. It also offers career advising, peer leadership, academic support and mental health and well-being programs.</p><p>This fall, the center opened a dedicated study space for veteran and military students and, with University Libraries, created a priority borrowing system for digital course materials, the university said.</p><p>"Our veterans and those currently serving in the National Guard, Reserve components and on Active Duty embody selfless service, and it is our honor to support them through every step of their higher education journey," center Director Lt. Col. John Fleming, a retired Marine, said in the release.</p><p>The center''s partners include the Brazos County Veteran Services Office, Military Admissions, University Health Services and the Texas A&amp;M Foundation, according to the university.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'The Texas Veterans Commission gave Texas A&M a gold-level 2026 Veteran Education Excellence Recognition Award, citing support for more than 1,600 veteran and military students.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-cv-10.webp', '2026-10-19T14:45:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Texas A&M','veterans','Texas Veterans Commission','student services','Brazos County']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Release does not say when the award was presented (''recently presented''); left undated.
• 확인할 점: Fleming''s class year (''94) and rank from the release; rendered as ''a retired Marine'' from ''USMC (Ret.)''.
• 확인할 점: Photo caption: Commons description says Secretary of Defense Robert Gates, his wife and former President George H. W. Bush were greeted with a Saber Arch on arrival in College Station, Oct. 26, 2007. That the arch was formed by Corps of Cadets members is a reasonable inference but not stated on the file page — editor may prefer ''A saber arch greets visiting dignitaries''. Photographer credited as DoD (individual name not shown on file page).
• 원문(Texas A&M Today — Texas A&M earns sixth gold award for veteran education excellence (Oct. 16, 2026)): https://stories.tamu.edu/news/2026/10/16/texas-am-earns-sixth-gold-award-for-veteran-education-excellence/
• 이용 조건: Text: facts and quote from Texas A&M University release, rewritten / Photo: Unsplash License, Iain Cridland / Unsplash, https://unsplash.com/photos/a-group-of-birds-flying-over-a-bridge-xtLDBRuViEY
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('local-news', 'published', 'College Station ISD Board to Weigh $154 Million Tax Levy, Custodial Raise', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — The College Station ISD Board of Trustees is set Tuesday to consider approving a 2026 property tax levy of about $154 million, a $1-an-hour raise for custodial workers and improvement plans for each of the district''s 19 campuses.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-09.webp" alt="The main entrance of A&amp;M Consolidated High School in College Station (file photo, 2016). Photo: Nicolás Macri / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)"><p><em>▲ The main entrance of A&amp;M Consolidated High School in College Station (file photo, 2016). Photo: Nicolás Macri / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)</em></p><p>The levy, the total amount of property tax the district will collect, is $154,029,193.18, based on a tax rate of $0.9702 per $100 of taxable value, according to the district. The Brazos County tax assessor-collector supplied the final tax roll and calculated the levy, and state law requires the board to approve it.</p><p>The rate is about half a cent lower than in 2025 because of required compression of the maintenance and operations rate, which pays for day-to-day costs, the district said. The interest and sinking rate, which repays voter-approved bonds, is unchanged. Even so, the levy is about $6 million higher than last year, mostly because taxable property values grew.</p><p>Trustees will also consider raising pay for custodial employees by $1 an hour and adjusting the custodial pay scale to match. The board is also expected to continue a discussion begun in September about having central office administrators serve as substitute teachers, and could act on a pilot program.</p><p>Other items include the 2026-27 district improvement plan, which focuses on academic and behavior support, middle school math and using student data to guide teaching, and purchases for projects funded by the 2023 bond, including equipment for the district''s new agriculture center.</p><p>The board will recognize the district''s eight National Merit Semifinalists, six from A&amp;M Consolidated High School and two from College Station High School. In the 5 p.m. workshop, Tom Maynard, who represents the area on the State Board of Education, is scheduled to update trustees on the board''s work.</p><p>The workshop begins at 5 p.m. and the regular meeting at 7 p.m. in the CSISD Boardroom on Welsh Avenue. Both will be streamed on the district''s YouTube channel.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'College Station ISD trustees meet Tuesday to consider a $154 million 2026 tax levy at a slightly lower rate, a $1-an-hour raise for custodians and improvement plans for all 19 campuses.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-09.webp', '2026-10-20T08:10:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['College Station ISD','school board','property tax','education']::text[], '• 확인할 점: This is a meeting preview dated the morning of the Oct. 20 meeting; outcomes (levy, custodial raise, substitute pilot) were not confirmed. Follow up with minutes or a district release.
• 확인할 점: Students'' names were in the district release but left out because they are minors.
• 확인할 점: The ''Before the Board'' post was not dated on the page itself; assumed published before Oct. 20.
• 확인할 점: Agriculture equipment purchase was listed at $120,449 from a named vendor; amount and vendor omitted for length.
• 원문(College Station ISD — Before The Board | Oct. 20, 2026): https://www.csisd.org/news/news-details/~board/college-station-independent-school-district-news/post/before-the-board-s-oct-20-2026-1792462748775
• 이용 조건: Text: facts from College Station ISD news post, rewritten / Photo: CC BY-SA 4.0 (also GFDL), Nicolás Macri, File:A&M Consolidated High School Main Entrance.jpg
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('texas-am', 'published', 'Space Force Picks Texas A&M to Train Up to 420 Captains a Year Through 2036', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Texas A&amp;M University will keep hosting the U.S. Space Force''s leadership course for captains through 2036, bringing as many as 420 officers a year to College Station, the university said.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-cv-09.webp" alt="Kyle Field on the Texas A&amp;M University campus in College Station (file photo, 2022). The Space Force course is not held there. Photo: David Rodezno / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ Kyle Field on the Texas A&amp;M University campus in College Station (file photo, 2022). The Space Force course is not held there. Photo: David Rodezno / Unsplash</em></p><p>Starting in fiscal year 2027, the four-week resident Captains Leadership Course will run up to seven cohorts of 60 students each, according to a Sept. 25 release from The Texas A&amp;M University System.</p><p>Texas A&amp;M was competitively selected in 2025 and has conducted six cohorts of the course this year, the release said. It is the Space Force''s first professional military education program built specifically for captains and run separately from Air Force instruction.</p><p>Space Force instructors teach space readiness and joint military integration. Faculty from Texas A&amp;M''s Bush School of Government and Public Service, working with the Corps of Cadets'' Hollingsworth Center for Ethical Leadership, teach leadership and communication, the university said. Space Training and Readiness Command, known as STARCOM, oversees the course through Space Delta 13.</p><p>"We have been honored to host the first six cohorts of the Captains Leadership Course, and we look forward to working with the Space Force to provide world-class instruction to many more Guardian officers," Bush School Dean John B. Sherman said in the release.</p><p>The partnership is formalized in a 10-year intergovernmental support agreement that also allows the two to pursue other training and education services, including cost-sharing arrangements, the university said.</p><p>Texas A&amp;M is one of six senior military colleges in the nation, according to the release.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'The U.S. Space Force will keep its Captains Leadership Course at Texas A&M through 2036, bringing up to seven cohorts of 60 officers a year to College Station for four weeks each.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-cv-09.webp', '2026-10-20T10:25:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Texas A&M','U.S. Space Force','Bush School','military','Corps of Cadets']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Release dated Sept. 25, 2026 (about a month old). Where the cohorts are housed/taught on campus and local economic impact were not stated — left out.
• 확인할 점: Course name: source uses ''Captains Leadership Course''; the Space Force photo title uses ''Captain''s Leadership Course''. Headline/body follow the university.
• 확인할 점: The release also quotes Regents Chairman Robert Albritton, President Susan Ballabina and Chancellor Glenn Hegar; only Sherman quoted here.
• 원문(Texas A&M Today — Texas A&M to prepare Space Force leaders through 2036 (Sept. 25, 2026)): https://stories.tamu.edu/news/2026/09/25/texas-am-to-prepare-space-force-leaders-through-2036/
• 이용 조건: Text: facts and quote from Texas A&M University System release, rewritten / Photo: Unsplash License, David Rodezno / Unsplash, https://unsplash.com/photos/a-large-brick-building-with-a-clock-on-the-front-of-it-MFy-8akrd6s
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('business', 'published', 'Brazos County Pay Rises 3.7% but Trails State, National Averages', '<p>BRYAN — Workers in Brazos County earned an average of $1,106 a week in the first three months of 2026, up 3.7%, or $39, from a year earlier, according to the latest county wage data from the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-bz-09.webp" alt="U.S. $100 bills (file photo). Photo: Giorgio Trovato / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ U.S. $100 bills (file photo). Photo: Giorgio Trovato / Unsplash</em></p><p>The county''s growth rate was close to the national gain of 3.9%, but the pay level remained well below the national average of $1,654 and the Texas average of $1,646, the data show.</p><p>The figures come from the Quarterly Census of Employment and Wages, a count of jobs covered by unemployment insurance. The county had 127,757 such jobs in March, up 262, or 0.2%, from March 2025, at 5,268 business locations.</p><p>Private employers added 764 jobs over the year, a 0.9% increase, to 83,584. Their average weekly wage rose 2.7% to $1,082. State government, which in Brazos County includes Texas A&amp;M University, employed 33,821 people, down 1.1%, while its average weekly wage climbed 6% to $1,145.</p><p>Pay varied widely by industry. Leisure and hospitality, which includes restaurants and hotels, employed 19,298 people at an average of $462 a week. Professional and business services paid an average of $1,752 a week, and manufacturing paid $1,185, up 7% from a year earlier.</p><p>The BLS calculates the average weekly wage by dividing total pay by the number of covered jobs, so part-time jobs are counted along with full-time ones. The agency has scheduled its second-quarter county figures for release Dec. 2.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Brazos County''s average weekly wage was $1,106 in the first quarter, up 3.7% from a year earlier, federal data show. That is well below the Texas average of $1,646 and the U.S. average of $1,654.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-bz-09.webp', '2026-10-20T10:30:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['wages','jobs','Brazos County','Bureau of Labor Statistics','labor market']::text[], '• 확인할 점: BLS released Q1 2026 county data Aug. 28, 2026; this is still the latest available. Editor may want to note it is not brand-new data.
• 확인할 점: Figures from QCEW open data CSV for area 48041 (Brazos County), 2026 Q1; U.S. (US000) and Texas (48000) totals from same API. All figures preliminary and subject to revision.
• 확인할 점: Statement that part-time workers are included is general QCEW methodology (wages per covered job); editor may trim.
• 확인할 점: Q2 release date Dec. 2, 2026 seen in BLS release text via search; bls.gov blocked direct access (403).
• 확인할 점: Calling Texas A&M part of ''state government'' ownership is our explanation; A&M is the county''s largest state employer but the data do not name employers.
• 원문(U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics — QCEW open data, Brazos County, Texas, Q1 2026): https://data.bls.gov/cew/data/api/2026/1/area/48041.csv
• 원문(U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics — QCEW open data, United States, Q1 2026): https://data.bls.gov/cew/data/api/2026/1/area/US000.csv
• 원문(U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics — QCEW open data, Texas, Q1 2026): https://data.bls.gov/cew/data/api/2026/1/area/48000.csv
• 원문(U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics — County Employment and Wages, First Quarter 2026 (Aug. 28, 2026)): https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/cewqtr_08282026.htm
• 이용 조건: Text: U.S. federal data (public domain), rewritten / Photo: Unsplash License, Giorgio Trovato / Unsplash, https://unsplash.com/photos/a-close-up-of-us-hundred-dollar-bills-fanned-out-P4xvs9snTno
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('aggie-football', 'published', 'Former Aggies Coach Jackie Sherrill to Receive Bear Bryant Lifetime Award', '<p>HOUSTON — Jackie Sherrill, the former Texas A&amp;M head football coach and athletics director, has been chosen to receive the American Heart Association''s 2027 Paul "Bear" Bryant Lifetime Achievement Award, Texas A&amp;M Athletics announced Tuesday.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-sp-01.webp" alt="The &quot;Home of the 12th Man&quot; entrance to Kyle Field in College Station (file photo, 2022). Photo: David Rodezno / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ The "Home of the 12th Man" entrance to Kyle Field in College Station (file photo, 2022). Photo: David Rodezno / Unsplash</em></p><p>Sherrill coached the Aggies for seven seasons, from 1982 through 1988, and went 52-28-1 overall and 36-17-1 in Southwest Conference play, according to the athletics department. His teams won three consecutive SWC championships from 1985 to 1987, going 20-3 in league games over that span, and he was named SWC Coach of the Year three times.</p><p>The 1985 conference title sent Texas A&amp;M to its first Cotton Bowl in nearly 20 years. The Aggies beat Auburn 36-16 in that game, holding down Heisman Trophy winner Bo Jackson, and made two more Cotton Bowl appearances, in 1987 and 1988. Those three teams finished ranked No. 6, No. 12 and No. 9 in the final Associated Press poll.</p><p>The release credits Sherrill with one of the most visible traditions at Kyle Field: the 12th Man Kickoff Team, a kickoff coverage unit made up of non-scholarship students. It allowed an average of 12.5 yards per return over its first five seasons, the school said.</p><p>"I knew that I could find, out of 40-some-odd thousand students, 11 guys that would cover kickoffs, but have no regard for their body and be tough as nails," Sherrill said, according to the release. "I just knew it would work."</p><p>The award has a personal link. Sherrill, a native of Duncan, Oklahoma, played for Bryant at Alabama from 1962 to 1965, when the Crimson Tide won two national championships, and later worked as a graduate assistant on Bryant''s staff. Bryant died of a heart attack in 1983, and the American Heart Association uses the annual awards to raise money and awareness for its work.</p><p>Sherrill is a member of the Texas A&amp;M Athletics Hall of Fame (2007) and was later inducted into the Mississippi, Alabama and Texas sports halls of fame. The 41st Bear Bryant Awards ceremony is scheduled for Wednesday, Jan. 27, in Houston and will air on CBS Sports Network. Bruce Arians was previously announced as the recipient of this year''s Heart of a Champion Award.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Jackie Sherrill, who led Texas A&M to three straight Southwest Conference titles and created the 12th Man Kickoff Team, will receive the 2027 Paul "Bear" Bryant Lifetime Achievement Award.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-sp-01.webp', '2026-10-20T13:15:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Texas A&M football','Jackie Sherrill','Bear Bryant Awards','12th Man','Aggie history']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Release says the Cotton Bowl win over Auburn came after the 1985 SWC title (game played Jan. 1, 1986); story avoids giving the game date.
• 확인할 점: Ceremony date is Wednesday, Jan. 27 (2027) per the release; confirm if the award organizers change it.
• 원문(Texas A&M Athletics — Sherrill To Receive Paul "Bear" Bryant Lifetime Achievement Award (Oct. 20, 2026)): https://12thman.com/news/2026/10/20/sherrill-to-receive-paul-bear-bryant-lifetime-achievement-award
• 이용 조건: Text: facts from Texas A&M Athletics (12thman.com) releases, rewritten; quotes as published by the athletics department / Photo: Unsplash License, David Rodezno / Unsplash, https://unsplash.com/photos/a-large-brick-building-with-a-sign-on-the-front-of-it-mxCyl1C5AGg
