  ('health-sleep', 'published', 'Cooler Days Make It Easier to Hit CDC''s 150-Minute Weekly Activity Goal', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — With the Brazos Valley''s summer heat behind it, fall is a good time to take another look at federal advice on how much physical activity adults need and what counts toward it.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-03.webp" alt="People walk along a park path covered in fallen leaves (file photo, 2026; taken in Bucharest, Romania, not in the Brazos Valley). Photo: Billy 4422 / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ People walk along a park path covered in fallen leaves (file photo, 2026; taken in Bucharest, Romania, not in the Brazos Valley). Photo: Billy 4422 / Unsplash</em></p><p>The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends that adults get at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic activity a week, such as brisk walking. That can be broken into 30 minutes a day, five days a week. Adults can instead do 75 minutes of vigorous activity, such as jogging or running, or a mix of the two.</p><p>The CDC also recommends muscle-strengthening activities at least two days a week that work all major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Adults 65 and older should follow the same aerobic and strength targets and add activities that improve balance, the agency says. Those who cannot meet the targets should be as active as their abilities and health conditions allow.</p><p>To judge effort, the CDC suggests a simple "talk test." During moderate activity, a person can talk but not sing. During vigorous activity, a person cannot say more than a few words without stopping for a breath. The agency counts walking at 2.5 miles per hour or faster, bicycling slower than 10 miles per hour on level ground, doubles tennis, line or ballroom dancing and general yard work as moderate activity.</p><p>The benefits start right away, according to the CDC, which says physical activity helps people feel better, function better and sleep better. Over time, the agency says, regular activity can lower the risk of heart disease, stroke, type 2 diabetes and at least eight types of cancer, and may reduce the risk of depression and anxiety.</p><p>Small changes add up. The CDC estimates that about 110,000 deaths a year could be prevented if U.S. adults 40 and older increased their activity, and says even 10 more minutes a day would make a difference.</p><p>People with chronic health conditions or disabilities can also benefit, the agency says, noting that activity can ease arthritis pain and help people with type 2 diabetes control their blood sugar.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'The CDC recommends adults get at least 150 minutes of moderate activity, such as brisk walking, each week plus two days of muscle strengthening. Even 10 more minutes a day helps, the agency says.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-03.webp', '2026-10-21T09:40:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['exercise','walking','CDC','physical activity','health']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Guidelines from CDC pages: adults (last reviewed Dec. 20, 2023), older adults (Dec. 4, 2025), measuring intensity (Dec. 4, 2025), benefits (Dec. 4, 2025).
• 확인할 점: The ''summer heat behind it'' lead is general framing, not a weather-data claim.
• 확인할 점: No local trail or program names are included; the editor could add city parks or trails if desired from official city pages.
• 확인할 점: Photo description on Commons: ''Rec Sports Complex at Texas A&M University Intramural Sports'' (May 22, 2016). Check the image for prominent faces.
• 원문(CDC — Adult Activity: An Overview (last reviewed Dec. 20, 2023)): https://www.cdc.gov/physical-activity-basics/guidelines/adults.html
• 원문(CDC — Older Adult Activity: An Overview (Dec. 4, 2025)): https://www.cdc.gov/physical-activity-basics/guidelines/older-adults.html
• 원문(CDC — Measuring Physical Activity Intensity (Dec. 4, 2025)): https://www.cdc.gov/physical-activity-basics/measuring/index.html
• 원문(CDC — Benefits of Physical Activity (Dec. 4, 2025)): https://www.cdc.gov/physical-activity-basics/benefits/index.html
