  ('food-dining', 'published', 'Food Trucks in College Station: What the City Requires Before Vendors Can Sell', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Food trucks, trailers and carts that sell in College Station must clear a list of city and county requirements before they open their windows, according to the city''s Mobile Food Vendor Guidebook.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-05.webp" alt="A food trailer with a service window and outdoor tables (file photo, 2023; taken in Tepic, Mexico, not in College Station). Photo: Alexiz Mora / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ A food trailer with a service window and outdoor tables (file photo, 2023; taken in Tepic, Mexico, not in College Station). Photo: Alexiz Mora / Unsplash</em></p><p>The city defines a mobile food vendor as any business selling food from a stationary spot in the city, including self-contained food trucks, concession trailers pulled by another vehicle and carts moved without a motor. Vendors selling only non-refrigerated farm products in their raw state are exempt, as are vendors working only at a city-permitted special event, though those still need a temporary food permit from the Brazos County Health District.</p><p>Applicants file through the city''s online permitting system, and the city says initial review takes about five business days. Applications must include the owner''s information, a sales tax permit, a diagram of where sales will happen, written permission from the property owner, proof of business auto liability insurance and a bond of at least $1,000. Before a permit is issued, the unit must pass an inspection by the College Station Fire Marshal''s Office, including a hood test for new vendors who cook food that produces greasy vapors.</p><p>Every mobile unit also needs a current food permit from the Brazos County Health District and must be tied to a central preparation facility, an inspected restaurant or commercial kitchen where the vendor cleans the vehicle, disposes of grease and waste, refills water and stores food. The guidebook says a private home cannot serve as that facility.</p><p>Location rules are detailed. Vendors may not sell in single-family residential or agricultural zoning districts, within 100 feet of the main entrance of an open restaurant outside Northgate, on streets classified as major collectors or larger, in bike lanes, or within 20 feet of another food truck unless they are in an approved mobile food court. In Northgate, the 100-foot buffer from a restaurant can be reduced with that restaurant''s permission.</p><p>Hours are limited, too. Vendors may not operate between 2 and 5 a.m., for more than five hours a day at one location, or for more than 30 minutes on smaller public streets. In the Northgate zoning districts, the daily limit per location is 21 hours.</p><p>On site, each unit must have two trash receptacles, keep five feet of clear space around it, post a "No Smoking" sign by the order window and keep a tagged fire extinguisher handy. Tables, chairs and canopies are not allowed, and neither are continuous music or flashing lights. City and county permits must be displayed where customers can read them.</p><p>Permits last one year, and renewals must be filed at least 30 days before expiration or the vendor must reapply as new, according to the guidebook. Mobile food courts, where two or more vendors share amenities, require a separate conditional use permit.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'College Station requires mobile food vendors to pass a fire inspection, carry a county health permit and follow location and hour limits, including no sales from 2 to 5 a.m., the city''s guidebook says.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-05.webp', '2026-10-22T13:10:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['food trucks','College Station','permits','Brazos County Health District']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Source is the City of College Station ''Mobile Food Vendor Guidebook'' PDF; the document is undated, so rules may have changed. Editor may want to confirm with Planning & Development Services that it is current.
• 확인할 점: Permit fees are in the city''s fee schedule and were not included (no prices). The $1,000 bond is a regulatory requirement stated in the guidebook.
• 확인할 점: The guidebook calls the county agency both ''Brazos County Health Department'' and ''Brazos County Health District''; the article uses ''Brazos County Health District''.
• 확인할 점: Hours rule wording: ''more than 30 minutes on any public street designated on the city''s Thoroughfare Plan as a minor collector or lesser'' simplified to ''smaller public streets''.
• 확인할 점: Email address and phone number in the guidebook are left out per style rules.
• 확인할 점: Not a news peg: this is an evergreen explainer. No new ordinance change was found.
• 확인할 점: Photo: CC0 photo of an ice cream food truck in Austin, Texas (2017). The truck may show a business name or brand; check the image and crop or replace if a company is singled out.
• 원문(City of College Station — Mobile Food Vendor Guidebook (PDF, undated)): https://www.cstx.gov/media/vscj4jzy/mobilefoodvendorpermitguide.pdf
• 이용 조건: Text: original wording based on facts in a City of College Station public document; no sentences copied / Photo: Unsplash License, Alexiz Mora / Unsplash, https://unsplash.com/photos/a-white-trailer-parked-next-to-a-table-and-chairs-RE7R7Aj1aew
