  ('latest', 'published', 'Doug Lyles Named BTU General Manager as Bryan Drops Interim Titles', '<p>BRYAN — Doug Lyles, who has led Bryan Texas Utilities on an interim basis since longtime general manager Gary Miller announced his retirement this summer, is now the utility''s permanent general manager, the City of Bryan announced.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-02.webp" alt="A lineman inspects storm-damaged power lines in Port Arthur, Texas, after Hurricane Ike (file photo, 2008). It does not show BTU crews or equipment. Photo: Liz Roll / FEMA / Wikimedia Commons (public domain)"><p><em>▲ A lineman inspects storm-damaged power lines in Port Arthur, Texas, after Hurricane Ike (file photo, 2008). It does not show BTU crews or equipment. Photo: Liz Roll / FEMA / Wikimedia Commons (public domain)</em></p><p>BTU is the city-owned electric utility. Lyles'' appointment was one of several leadership changes the city released Oct. 22, including the removal of interim designations for two other top posts and promotions in technology, facilities and economic development.</p><p>Lyles has worked in the Texas electric industry for 25 years, the last 17 of them at BTU, according to the city. He has served there as controller, manager of financial planning, chief risk officer and executive director of business and customer operations. He is a certified public accountant and holds bachelor''s and master''s degrees from Texas A&amp;M University.</p><p>Meagan Brown, who had been interim executive director of customer operations at BTU, is now executive director of customer relations. The city said she will oversee customer service, communications, energy services, key accounts and distributed generation programs.</p><p>Alicia Kenney, interim chief financial officer since April, is now the city''s chief financial officer while she completes her CPA designation. Kenney has worked for the city since 2015 and leads its finance department, which handles budgeting, accounting, treasury, debt and financial reporting for both the city and BTU.</p><p>The city also promoted Bill Bullock to IT director and named Daniel Adam director of business innovation and applications. Frank Clark, who has managed projects at Travis Bryan Midtown Park including the Legends Event Center, became director of special projects and facilities. Lauren Hovde moved from development services to the economic development department as economic development coordinator.</p><p>"These promotions and staff updates ensure long-term coordination and stability across city departments, and improve our ability to execute the vision of the City Council," City Manager Andrew Nelson said in the release.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Bryan has made Doug Lyles permanent general manager of city-owned Bryan Texas Utilities and removed interim titles for its chief financial officer and a BTU customer operations leader.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-02.webp', '2026-10-22T15:40:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Bryan','Bryan Texas Utilities','BTU','city government','utilities']::text[], '• 확인할 점: The release does not give effective dates or salaries for the appointments; none stated.
• 확인할 점: The release says Miller ''announced his retirement this summer''; the earlier city release on his retirement (on bryantx.gov news page 4, July 2026) was not opened, so no retirement date is given.
• 확인할 점: Lyles'' degrees: BBA in accounting and MS in finance, both Texas A&M, per release.
• 원문(City of Bryan — City of Bryan Removes Interim Designations & Announces Staff Updates (Oct. 22, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/city-of-bryan-removes-interim-designations-announces-staff-updates/
• 이용 조건: Text: facts from City of Bryan news release, rewritten; one quote from the release / Photo: public domain (PD-USGov-FEMA), Liz Roll/FEMA, File:FEMA - 38497 - A lineman checks downed power lines in Texas.jpg
