  ('business', 'published', 'College Station Moves to Finalize Deal for The Station Sports Complex', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — The College Station City Council has directed city staff to finalize an economic development agreement with The Station, a planned regional sports complex in the city''s Midtown Business Park, Mayor John Nichols announced at the council''s Oct. 22 meeting.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-bz-03.webp" alt="A youth baseball field (file photo, 1972; not in College Station). Photo: William C. Shrout, U.S. National Archives / Wikimedia Commons (public domain)"><p><em>▲ A youth baseball field (file photo, 1972; not in College Station). Photo: William C. Shrout, U.S. National Archives / Wikimedia Commons (public domain)</em></p><p>The direction came after a closed executive session, according to the city''s live blog of the meeting. The final agreement has not been made public, and the blog did not say when it would return to the council for a vote.</p><p>The project has been moving through city approvals since February, when the council unanimously approved a nonbinding letter of intent with the developer. The concept is a sports resort with nine synthetic turf baseball and softball fields, including a championship-level field designed for tournaments and Brazos Valley Bombers home games, plus cabins, RV spaces and other amenities for visitors, the city said at the time.</p><p>City documents in February said that if the city went ahead, its share would include a $20 million cash incentive, land incentives and construction of infrastructure to serve the site. Preliminary estimates put the complex''s annual economic impact at about $28 million, mostly from tournament visitors, according to the city.</p><p>The letter of intent called for the developer to submit a business plan, financial statements and an independent market study so the city could judge the project''s viability before signing a formal Chapter 380 agreement, the city said.</p><p>On Aug. 27, the council unanimously changed the land-use designation of 106 acres of city-owned property east of Midtown Drive and Corporate Parkway from business center to general commercial and rezoned 93 acres from light industrial to general commercial to make room for the project. It also removed a planned extension of Corporate Parkway between Midtown Drive and William D. Fitch Parkway from city transportation plans because the complex would block it.</p><p>The site is near the 28 acres where the city agreed in September to sell land for a Bass Pro Shops store and a mixed-use development.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'College Station''s council told staff Oct. 22 to finalize an economic development agreement for The Station, a planned baseball and softball complex with lodging on city land in Midtown.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-bz-03.webp', '2026-10-23T10:20:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['College Station','The Station','sports tourism','Midtown','economic development']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Only the council''s direction to finalize was announced Oct. 22; final incentive amounts and acreage are not public. The $20 million / $28 million figures are from the Feb. 26 LOI description and may change.
• 확인할 점: City blog posts are ''not the official minutes''.
• 확인할 점: Sources describe the developer differently: ''The Station Hospitality and Athletic Complex'' (Feb. 26 blog); news reposts say ''The Station Baseball LLC''. We avoided naming the legal entity.
• 확인할 점: ''Near the 28 acres'' is based on the Sept. 24 Grow College Station release saying the Bass Pro site sits in front of the planned South College Station baseball and softball complex.
• 확인할 점: Photo: EPA Documerica (1972), federal work, public domain via NARA.
• 원문(City of College Station blog — Live from City Hall: Thursday''s City Council meeting (Oct. 22, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/10/22/live-from-city-hall-thursdays-city-council-meeting-oct-22/
• 원문(City of College Station blog — Live from City Hall: Thursday''s City Council meeting (Aug. 27, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/08/27/live-from-city-hall-thursdays-city-council-meeting-aug-27/
• 원문(City of College Station blog — Live from City Hall: Thursday''s City Council meeting (Feb. 26, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/02/26/live-from-city-hall-thursdays-city-council-meeting-feb-26/
• 원문(Grow College Station — City Council approves agreements with Bass Pro Shops, Corinth Properties (Sept. 24, 2026)): https://grow.cstx.gov/news/posts/city-council-approves-agreements-with-bass-pro-shops-corinth-properties/
• 이용 조건: Text: facts rewritten from public government sources; no text copied / Photo: public domain (U.S. federal work, EPA Documerica via NARA), William C. Shrout, File:THIS LITTLE LEAGUE BASEBALL FIELD WAS BUILT ON SANITARY LANDFILL - NARA - 546272.jpg
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('health-sleep', 'published', 'Ragweed Season Can Run Into November; Allergists Say Keep Up Treatment', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Cooler mornings have arrived in the Brazos Valley, but for people with fall allergies the sneezing may not be over. Ragweed, the main fall pollen, can keep releasing pollen until a hard frost, according to federal health researchers and a national allergy group.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-02.webp" alt="Common ragweed with flower spikes (file photo, 2022; not taken in the Brazos Valley). Photo: GoranH / Pixabay"><p><em>▲ Common ragweed with flower spikes (file photo, 2022; not taken in the Brazos Valley). Photo: GoranH / Pixabay</em></p><p>Ragweed pollen season runs from August to November and typically peaks in mid-September in many areas, according to the National Institute of Environmental Health Sciences, part of the National Institutes of Health. The institute calls ragweed one of the most prolific pollen producers and says seasonal allergies, also known as hay fever, affect nearly one in six Americans.</p><p>The American College of Allergy, Asthma and Immunology, a professional group of allergists, says ragweed traditionally blooms in late summer and early fall but that lingering warm weather is stretching the season. In an Aug. 1, 2025, release, the college cited the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency as saying the nation''s frost-free season has grown by more than two weeks on average.</p><p>"The prolonged warm weather means that plants bloom earlier, stay around longer and produce more pollen," said allergist James Tracy, DO, who was the college''s president at the time of the release.</p><p>To limit exposure, the environmental health institute recommends avoiding time outdoors between 5 and 10 a.m., planning outdoor activities for late afternoon or after a heavy rain, keeping home and car windows closed and using air conditioning instead of window or attic fans. It notes that pollen can ride indoors on people and pets and suggests wearing a mask while mowing.</p><p>The allergy college advises changing out of outdoor clothes, leaving shoes at the door and showering after time outside, with attention to hair. For yard work, it suggests a hat, sunglasses and an N95 mask rated by the National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health.</p><p>Timing matters for medicine, too. The college recommends starting allergy medicine about two weeks before symptoms usually begin and continuing for about two weeks after the first hard frost. The institute says antihistamines and nasal steroid sprays can ease hay fever symptoms.</p><p>People whose symptoms have gotten worse or changed, or who are coughing or wheezing more than usual, should see a board-certified allergist and ask about asthma testing, the college says. It notes that allergy shots or tablets can provide long-term relief for severe or chronic allergies.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Ragweed pollen season runs from August into November, federal researchers say. Allergists advise continuing medicine for about two weeks after the first hard frost and limiting morning time outdoors.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-02.webp', '2026-10-23T10:20:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['allergies','ragweed','pollen','health']::text[], '• 확인할 점: No local pollen count or Brazos Valley first-frost date is included. An official NWS average first-freeze date for College Station could not be found on weather.gov; other figures (about Nov. 29) came from news outlets and were not used.
• 확인할 점: The ACAAI release is from Aug. 1, 2025; James Tracy was ACAAI president at that time. The text says ''at the time of the release'' because his current title was not checked.
• 확인할 점: NIEHS pollen page last reviewed March 6, 2026. The 5 to 10 a.m. window and the ''nearly one in six Americans'' figure are from that page.
• 확인할 점: No medical claims beyond the sources. The article does not name medicines or brands.
• 확인할 점: The ACAAI release mentions its ''Find an Allergist'' tool; it is left out (no URLs or promotion).
• 원문(NIEHS — Pollen (last reviewed March 6, 2026)): https://www.niehs.nih.gov/health/topics/agents/allergens/pollen
• 원문(American College of Allergy, Asthma and Immunology — 5 Expert Ways to Prepare for a Longer Fall Allergy Season (Aug. 1, 2025)): https://acaai.org/news/5-expert-ways-to-prepare-for-a-longer-fall-allergy-season/
• 이용 조건: Text: original wording; NIEHS is a U.S. federal source (public domain); ACAAI facts and one quote used with attribution, no sentences copied / Photo: Pixabay Content License, GoranH / Pixabay, https://pixabay.com/photos/common-ragweed-annual-ragweed-7430352/
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('bryan', 'published', 'Bryan Parks App Handles Sign-Ups as Youth Basketball, Senior Socials Near', '<p>BRYAN — Residents signing up for City of Bryan recreation programs this fall can do it from a phone, using a parks and recreation app the city launched in August.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-cv-03.webp" alt="An outdoor basketball hoop (file photo). It is not a Bryan park facility. Photo: Ray Shrewsberry / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ An outdoor basketball hoop (file photo). It is not a Bryan park facility. Photo: Ray Shrewsberry / Unsplash</em></p><p>The free Bryan Parks &amp; Recreation app, available for iPhone and Android, lets users register and pay for programs, rent pavilions and tennis or pickleball courts, and manage pool passes and tennis center memberships, the city said in an Aug. 19 news release. It uses the same account as the city''s parks registration website, and phone and in-person registration remain available.</p><p>The city said staff can also use the app to send alerts about weather, registration deadlines and other program changes.</p><p>Several fall programs listed in the city''s seasonal schedule are coming up. Registration for Start Smart Basketball, for children ages 3 to 5, closes Nov. 4, with sessions on Tuesdays from Nov. 10 through Dec. 15.</p><p>For adults 55 and older, free Senior Socials that do not require registration are scheduled for Nov. 10 and Dec. 8. A new evening series, Senior Socials After Hours, has paid programs on Nov. 19 and Dec. 10, and the free weekly Games &amp; Grounds gathering continues on Thursdays through Nov. 12.</p><p>Water Fit classes on Tuesdays and Thursdays and Aqua Boot Camp on Mondays and Wednesdays run through Nov. 19, according to the schedule. The Neal Recreation Center''s after-school program and holiday camp for ages 5 to 12 accept registration on an ongoing basis and follow the Bryan ISD calendar.</p><p>The city said its fall programs have no residency requirement. Bryan''s Parks, Recreation &amp; Facility Services Department manages 51 parks covering more than 870 acres, according to the department''s website.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Bryan''s new parks and recreation app lets residents register and reserve courts. Start Smart basketball sign-ups close Nov. 4, and free senior socials continue Nov. 10 and Dec. 8.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-cv-03.webp', '2026-10-24T09:40:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Bryan','parks and recreation','seniors','youth sports','city services']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Program dates come from the city''s ''Fall 2026 Parks & Recreation at a Glance'' release dated Aug. 24, 2026; schedules may have changed since. Confirm dates before publication if possible.
• 확인할 점: Fees deliberately omitted (Senior Socials After Hours are described only as ''paid programs'').
• 확인할 점: App description is from the Aug. 19, 2026 release; app store names given only as iPhone/Android, no download instructions.
• 확인할 점: 51 parks / 870 acres figure from the department''s landing page (read Oct. 27, 2026).
• 원문(City of Bryan — City Launches New App for Parks & Recreation Programs (Aug. 19, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/city-launches-new-app-for-parks-recreation-programs/
• 원문(City of Bryan — Fall 2026 Parks & Recreation at a Glance (Aug. 24, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/fall-2026-parks-recreation-at-a-glance/
• 원문(City of Bryan — Parks, Recreation & Facility Services department page (read Oct. 27, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/parks-and-recreation/
