  ('food-dining', 'published', 'Persimmon Named Newest Texas Superstar Plant by Texas A&M AgriLife', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — The orange fruit that ripens on backyard trees each fall has earned a statewide endorsement. Texas A&amp;M AgriLife has named the Oriental persimmon, also called the Japanese persimmon, its newest Texas Superstar plant, AgriLife Today reported Oct. 19.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-06.webp" alt="Ripe persimmons on a tree branch (file photo, 2022; not taken in Texas). Photo: Sharlene Rochen / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ Ripe persimmons on a tree branch (file photo, 2022; not taken in Texas). Photo: Sharlene Rochen / Unsplash</em></p><p>The Texas Superstar label goes only to plants that have performed reliably in Texas after years of field trials by Texas A&amp;M AgriLife Research and the Texas A&amp;M AgriLife Extension Service, the agency said. Plants are judged on appearance, reliability and how well they handle the state''s range of soils and temperatures.</p><p>Larry Stein, an AgriLife Extension horticulture specialist and professor in the Texas A&amp;M Department of Horticultural Sciences, based in Uvalde, ranked persimmons among the most consistent, low-maintenance fruit trees for Texas homeowners. "Persimmons are a great addition around the home," Stein said.</p><p>Persimmons come in two types, according to AgriLife. Astringent varieties that do well in Texas, including Eureka, Hachiya, Tani-nashi and Tamopan, must be fully ripe and soft before they are pleasant to eat, a process often helped by cooler weather. The non-astringent Fuyu can be eaten as soon as its color changes, while still firm.</p><p>The fruit is very sweet, AgriLife said. Non-astringent varieties measure 15 to 18 on the Brix scale used for sugar content, and astringent types reach 18 to 25. By comparison, watermelons are considered extra sweet at 11 to 12. Persimmons are also high in vitamin A.</p><p>The Oriental persimmon is often confused with the native Texas persimmon, which produces a small purple fruit that is edible but much smaller and lower in quality, according to AgriLife. Trees sold in Texas are usually grafted onto rootstock from the American persimmon, a native of the eastern United States.</p><p>For those who want to grow their own, AgriLife says fall to winter is the best time to plant. The trees typically grow 10 to 15 feet tall, do best in full sun and well-drained soil, and should be spaced 12 to 15 feet apart. They have few major insect or disease problems and need little care once established.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Texas A&M AgriLife named the Oriental persimmon its newest Texas Superstar plant, citing a small, low-care tree whose orange fall fruit is very sweet and high in vitamin A.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-06.webp', '2026-10-24T11:30:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['persimmon','Texas A&M AgriLife','Texas Superstar','fruit']::text[], '• 확인할 점: All facts from AgriLife Today, ''Persimmon named newest Texas Superstar plant'' by Adam Russell (Oct. 19, 2026). Quote is as it appears in the source.
• 확인할 점: The AgriLife story says Texas trees are ''typically grafted'' onto American persimmon rootstock; the article says ''Trees sold in Texas are usually grafted'' — editor may prefer the source''s wording (''Texas trees are typically grafted'').
• 확인할 점: Texas Superstar is a registered trademark of AgriLife Research (mentioned in source; not needed in text).
• 확인할 점: No prices, nurseries or purchase language included.
• 확인할 점: AgriLife Today is a Texas A&M publication (copyrighted text); facts rewritten, no sentences copied.
• 원문(AgriLife Today — Persimmon named newest Texas Superstar plant (Oct. 19, 2026)): https://agrilifetoday.tamu.edu/2026/10/19/persimmon-named-newest-texas-superstar-plant/
• 이용 조건: Text: original wording based on facts from Texas A&M AgriLife Today / Photo: Unsplash License, Sharlene Rochen / Unsplash, https://unsplash.com/photos/a-group-of-fruits-on-a-tree-09eq_1KZrag
