  ('opinion', 'draft', 'Our View: A Wet El Niño Winter Is No Reason to Stop Saving Water', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — After a summer when Bryan and College Station asked residents to ease up on sprinklers, the forecast finally sounds like good news. Texas State Climatologist John Nielsen-Gammon says an unusually strong El Niño is building in the Pacific, and El Niños generally bring wetter weather to Texas from October through April. This newspaper hopes the rain comes. But we urge residents and both cities not to treat one wet winter as the end of the water conversation.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-10.webp" alt="A lawn sprinkler watering grass (file photo, 2018; not taken in Bryan or College Station). Photo: Methi SOMÇAĞ / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ A lawn sprinkler watering grass (file photo, 2018; not taken in Bryan or College Station). Photo: Methi SOMÇAĞ / Unsplash</em></p><p>Consider where we stand. Most of Texas remained in drought on the U.S. Drought Monitor map in mid-October, even after recent storms, Texas A&amp;M AgriLife reported Oct. 20. Ronnie Schnell, a state cropping systems specialist with AgriLife, warned that the moisture in many wet fields was only at the surface. "We do have wet fields in places right now, but it''s surface, so it''s not deep moisture," he said.</p><p>Consider, too, what may come next. Nielsen-Gammon said outlooks show this El Niño peaking around December and weakening through spring, with a good chance that La Niña develops next year. "So, this is probably a one-shot deal," he said. La Niña''s pattern is the opposite of El Niño''s, and drought-weary Texans know what that can mean for the following summer.</p><p>Locally, the warning signs came in late August, when the Bryan and College Station water systems said demand had risen rapidly with triple-digit temperatures and asked residents to water outdoors no more than twice a week and avoid daytime watering. Neither city imposed restrictions, a sign that voluntary conservation can work when people pay attention.</p><p>So let''s keep paying attention. Residents can turn off sprinklers when it rains, fix leaks and follow the weekly watering guidance the cities promote. And the cities can use a wetter winter to plan, not relax: reporting how much water was saved this year, how close demand came to capacity and what a dry 2027 would require. The cheapest water is the water we never have to pump.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Draft editorial: Forecasters expect a strong El Niño to bring a wetter Texas winter, but La Niña may follow. Bryan and College Station residents should keep conserving water.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-10.webp', null::timestamptz, array['editorial','water conservation','drought','El Niño','Brazos Valley']::text[], '• 확인할 점: AI DRAFT: This is an AI-written draft editorial. The editor must rewrite it in the paper''s own voice and decide the position before publishing. Do not publish as is.
• 확인할 점: Climate facts and both quotes from AgriLife Today, ''Super El Niño brings hope for moisture to drought-weary Texas'' by Adam Russell (Oct. 20, 2026). The story gives no Brazos Valley-specific drought data.
• 확인할 점: ''La Niña''s pattern is the opposite of El Niño''s, and drought-weary Texans know what that can mean'' is general background/opinion, not stated in the source. Editor may want to cite NOAA or the state climatologist on La Niña and Texas drought, or soften.
• 확인할 점: Local water facts from the joint Bryan/College Station release of Aug. 26, 2026 (no restrictions in place; twice-weekly outdoor watering guidance). No newer local water status was checked; confirm no restrictions or new releases since then.
• 확인할 점: The release mentions BVWaterSmart.org weekly guidance; the website name is left out of the text (no URLs).
• 확인할 점: The photo is a Texas A&M campus water tower; the university runs its own water system separate from the cities''. Editor may prefer a different image.
• 확인할 점: Last line is opinion.
• 원문(AgriLife Today — Super El Niño brings hope for moisture to drought-weary Texas (Oct. 20, 2026)): https://agrilifetoday.tamu.edu/2026/10/20/super-el-nino-brings-hope-for-moisture-to-drought-weary-texas/
• 원문(Cities of College Station and Bryan — Area water systems recommend conservation efforts due to summer heat (Aug. 26, 2026)): https://www.cstx.gov/news/posts/area-water-systems-recommend-conservation-efforts-due-to-summer-heat/
• 이용 조건: Text: original opinion draft based on public sources (AgriLife Today facts and quotes with attribution; city release) / Photo: Unsplash License, Methi SOMÇAĞ / Unsplash, https://unsplash.com/photos/AymI2mt9wRY
• 상태: 의견 기사라 작성 중(초안). 편집장이 고쳐 쓴 뒤 발행'),
  ('health-sleep', 'published', 'Only 1 in 4 High School Students Gets 8 Hours of Sleep, CDC Survey Finds', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Most high school students across the country are not getting the sleep doctors recommend on school nights, according to the latest national survey data from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, and the agency says phones and other screens in the bedroom are part of the problem.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-01.webp" alt="A person using a smartphone on a bed in a darkened room (file photo, 2021; posed stock image, not a Brazos Valley student). Photo: cottonbro studio / Pexels"><p><em>▲ A person using a smartphone on a bed in a darkened room (file photo, 2021; posed stock image, not a Brazos Valley student). Photo: cottonbro studio / Pexels</em></p><p>In 2025, 24% of U.S. high school students said they slept at least eight hours on an average school night, the CDC reported on a Youth Risk Behavior Survey data page updated Aug. 14. That is about one student in four. The share was unchanged from 2023 but lower than the 27% recorded in 2015.</p><p>The gap widens as students get older. According to the CDC, 31% of ninth graders reported getting at least eight hours, compared with 25% of 10th graders and 20% of students in both 11th and 12th grades. Boys were slightly more likely than girls to meet the mark, 26% to 22%.</p><p>The American Academy of Sleep Medicine recommends that teenagers ages 13 to 18 sleep eight to 10 hours a night, the CDC notes. The National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute, part of the National Institutes of Health, gives the same range for teens and recommends nine to 12 hours for children ages 6 to 12.</p><p>For families, the CDC recommends keeping wake-up times and bedtimes consistent, including on weekends, and says teenagers whose parents set bedtimes tend to sleep more. It also suggests setting nighttime limits on digital media, such as putting all devices away at least an hour before bed, and keeping phones outside the bedroom overnight. The agency says screens in bedrooms have been linked to shorter sleep, later bedtimes and poorer sleep quality.</p><p>The CDC also encourages schools to teach about sleep in health classes, including device use before bed and drowsy driving, and to look at how school start times affect students'' rest.</p><p>Adults are not off the hook. The CDC says adults ages 18 to 60 need seven or more hours a night and recommends turning off electronic devices at least 30 minutes before bedtime, keeping the bedroom quiet and cool, and avoiding caffeine in the afternoon or evening.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'New CDC survey data show just 24% of U.S. high school students slept at least eight hours on a school night in 2025. The agency urges families to set device limits before bed.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-01.webp', '2026-10-26T08:15:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['sleep','teens','screens','CDC','health']::text[], '• 확인할 점: All survey figures are from the CDC YRBS ''Sleep'' page (Youth Health in Focus), which says it was last updated Aug. 14, 2026 and reviewed Sept. 18, 2026. Figures are national; no Brazos County or Texas breakdown was used.
• 확인할 점: The trend line on the CDC page shows 27% (2015), 25%, 22%, 23%, 23%, 24% (2025). The article only cites 2015, 2023 and 2025.
• 확인할 점: The ''devices away at least 1 hour before bed'' advice is the CDC''s for adolescents; the ''30 minutes'' advice is from the CDC general sleep page (dated May 15, 2024). Both are attributed separately.
• 확인할 점: NHLBI sleep amounts from its ''How Much Sleep Is Enough'' page (last updated March 24, 2022).
• 확인할 점: No local school-district data or bell times were used. A local angle (Bryan ISD / CSISD start times or sleep education) could be added by the editor if confirmed with the districts.
• 확인할 점: Photo is an illustration (not a real person); it shows a woman with a phone in bed. Check that it reads as an illustration in the layout.
• 원문(CDC — Youth Risk Behavior Surveillance System: Sleep (updated Aug. 14, 2026)): https://www.cdc.gov/yrbs/youth-health-in-focus/sleep.html
• 원문(CDC — About Sleep (May 15, 2024)): https://www.cdc.gov/sleep/about/index.html
• 원문(NHLBI — How Much Sleep Is Enough? (March 24, 2022)): https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep/how-much-sleep
• 이용 조건: Text: original wording based on CDC and NHLBI pages (U.S. federal works, public domain) / Photo: Pexels License, cottonbro studio / Pexels, https://www.pexels.com/photo/a-man-using-a-phone-while-on-the-bed-6943444/
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('latest', 'published', 'College Station Road Work: Marion Pugh, Fitch Parkway Projects Run Into Winter', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Several College Station road projects will keep affecting drivers into the winter, with the Marion Pugh Drive rebuild expected to last until early January and the William D. Fitch Parkway rehabilitation set to finish by mid-December, according to city updates.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-07.webp" alt="Detour and road-closed signs at an intersection in New Knoxville, Ohio (file photo, 2015). It does not show College Station. Photo: Nyttend / Wikimedia Commons (public domain)"><p><em>▲ Detour and road-closed signs at an intersection in New Knoxville, Ohio (file photo, 2015). It does not show College Station. Photo: Nyttend / Wikimedia Commons (public domain)</em></p><p>On Marion Pugh, near Texas A&amp;M''s west side, crews began the project''s Phase 3A in September, including demolition, waterline work, new road and sidewalk on the west side of the street and drainage improvements at Luther Street, the city said. Luther Street remains closed throughout that phase, which the city estimated would take 10 to 12 weeks.</p><p>Work will then move to the east side of Marion Pugh for about six to eight weeks, with traffic limited to one southbound lane. The city said it is coordinating with Texas A&amp;M to adjust traffic control for home football games and urged drivers to expect delays and watch for crews.</p><p>On William D. Fitch Parkway, the final section of the rehabilitation, from Williams Creek Drive to Rock Prairie Road, began Sept. 9. Two-way traffic is being maintained, including access to the Williams Creek subdivision, and the project is expected to be finished by mid-December.</p><p>In south College Station, the south side of the Krenek Tap Road and Dartmouth Street intersection closed Oct. 19 for about two weeks of roadwork. The city said northbound Dartmouth traffic is detoured by way of FM 2818 and Texas Avenue, and southbound traffic by way of Krenek Tap Road and the State Highway 6 frontage road.</p><p>In early October, the city also scheduled weeklong closures on Holleman Drive South, between Rock Prairie and Graham roads, and on Finfeather Road, between Yegua Street and F&amp;B Road, for stormwater pipe work.</p><p>City officials have asked drivers to plan routes ahead of time, allow extra travel time and follow detour signs in work zones.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'College Station drivers face months more work on Marion Pugh Drive and William D. Fitch Parkway, while part of the Dartmouth Street-Krenek Tap Road intersection is closed for about two weeks.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-07.webp', '2026-10-26T08:40:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['College Station','road construction','traffic','Marion Pugh Drive','William D. Fitch Parkway']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Schedules are from city releases dated Sept. 8 (Fitch), Sept. 17 (Marion Pugh) and Oct. 16 (Dartmouth-Krenek Tap); no newer updates were found on cstx.gov news. Dates may have shifted; check before publication.
• 확인할 점: Holleman/Finfeather closures were scheduled to start Oct. 5 for about a week; no completion notice was found, so the story says only that they were scheduled.
• 확인할 점: Dartmouth-Krenek Tap: release said ''Beginning Monday'' on Friday, Oct. 16, so start date is Oct. 19; end date about Nov. 2 is not stated in the story.
• 확인할 점: Marion Pugh location ''near Texas A&M''s west side'' is general background, not from the release.
• 확인할 점: Brazos County and TxDOT Bryan District road pages could not be read (county site blocks automated access; TxDOT page showed no releases), so no county/state projects included.
• 원문(City of College Station — Marion Pugh construction update (Sept. 17, 2026)): https://www.cstx.gov/news/posts/marion-pugh-construction-update-sept-21/
• 원문(City of College Station — Final section of William D. Fitch Parkway rehabilitation begins Wednesday (Sept. 8, 2026)): https://www.cstx.gov/news/posts/final-section-of-william-d-fitch-parkway-rehabilitation-begins-wednesday/
• 원문(City of College Station — South side of Dartmouth-Krenek Tap intersection to be closed for two weeks (Oct. 16, 2026)): https://www.cstx.gov/news/posts/south-side-of-dartmouth-krenek-tap-intersection-to-be-closed-for-two-weeks/
• 원문(City of College Station — Road closures scheduled starting Monday on Holleman South and Finfeather (Oct. 2, 2026)): https://www.cstx.gov/news/posts/road-closures-scheduled-starting-monday-on-holleman-south-and-finfeather/
• 이용 조건: Text: facts from City of College Station releases, rewritten / Photo: public domain (PD-self), Nyttend, File:Detour through closed road.jpg
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('bryan', 'published', 'Bryan Animal Center Sets Free Pet Microchip, Rabies Vaccine Event for Nov. 14', '<p>BRYAN — Bryan pet owners can get their dogs and cats microchipped and vaccinated against rabies at no cost Saturday, Nov. 14, at the Bryan Animal Center''s next community event.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-cv-02.webp" alt="A veterinarian gives a dog an injection (file photo). It is not from a Bryan Animal Center event. Photo: Pranidchakan Boonrom / Pexels"><p><em>▲ A veterinarian gives a dog an injection (file photo). It is not from a Bryan Animal Center event. Photo: Pranidchakan Boonrom / Pexels</em></p><p>The event runs from 8 a.m. to noon, according to the animal center''s page on the City of Bryan website. It is open to animals owned by Bryan residents.</p><p>The city holds the free events four times a year, in March, May, August and November, rotating among city parks so they are easier for residents across Bryan to reach, the city said in an April news release. Location and registration details are posted on the Animal Center''s webpage.</p><p>"Vaccines and microchips can be expensive for pet owners," Animal Services Supervisor Ashley Bishop said in the release. "We don''t want cost holding people back from keeping their pets safe and healthy."</p><p>Texas law requires rabies vaccinations for dogs and cats every one to three years, depending on the vaccine, the city said. The city''s events offer one-year vaccines. Microchips are not required, but they give a pet permanent identification that can lead an animal control officer back to the owner.</p><p>As of April, the program had given 2,155 rabies vaccinations and 1,797 microchips in the six years since the free events began, the city said. The microchips went to dogs, cats and one macaw. Local veterinarian Dr. Daniel Hendrix has volunteered to give the rabies vaccines since the start, according to the city.</p><p>Bishop also encouraged owners to update their pet''s microchip contact information at least once a year. The center lists its following free event for March 13, 2027.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'The Bryan Animal Center will offer free microchips and rabies vaccinations for pets of Bryan residents from 8 a.m. to noon Nov. 14, part of a program held four times a year.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-cv-02.webp', '2026-10-26T11:05:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Bryan','Bryan Animal Center','pets','rabies vaccination','microchips']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Date and time (Nov. 14, 8 a.m.-noon) come from the Animal Center page''s event list; the event detail page on events.bryantx.gov returned 403, so the park location for Nov. 14 and any registration requirement were NOT confirmed and are left out.
• 확인할 점: Program statistics (2,155 vaccines, 1,797 microchips) and quotes are from the city''s April 27, 2026 release; totals have likely grown since the May and August events.
• 확인할 점: Eligibility wording (''animals owned by Bryan residents'') is from the April release; confirm it still applies.
• 원문(City of Bryan — Bryan Animal Center page, upcoming events (read Oct. 27, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/animal-services/animal-center/
• 원문(City of Bryan — Bryan Animal Center Serves the Community With Free Microchips & Rabies Vaccinations (Apr. 27, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/bryan-animal-center-serves-the-community-with-free-microchips-rabies-vaccinations/
• 이용 조건: Text: facts and quotes from City of Bryan releases, rewritten / Photo: Pexels License, Pranidchakan Boonrom / Pexels, https://www.pexels.com/photo/vet-vaccinating-adult-tan-pit-bull-terrier-1350591/
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('local-news', 'published', 'Bryan-College Station Libraries Set Author Visit, Friends Fall Book Sale', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — The Bryan + College Station Public Library System has two public events on its calendar in early November: a visit by bestselling author Elle Cosimano and the Friends of the Library''s fall book sale.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-10.webp" alt="Used books on a table at a book market in Milan, Italy (file photo, 2017). It is not the Friends of the Library sale. Photo: Tom Hermans / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ Used books on a table at a book market in Milan, Italy (file photo, 2017). It is not the Friends of the Library sale. Photo: Tom Hermans / Unsplash</em></p><p>Cosimano will speak at 6 p.m. Wednesday, Nov. 4, at the Larry J. Ringer Library on Harvey Mitchell Parkway South in College Station as part of the library system''s 2026 Author Series, which is sponsored by the Friends of the Library. The event includes a presentation, a question-and-answer session and a book signing, and admission is free, the library said.</p><p>The first 50 people who register and attend in person will receive a copy of her novel "Finlay Donovan is Killing It," according to the library. Doors open at least 30 minutes before the start.</p><p>Cosimano is a New York Times and USA Today bestselling author who has also written young adult novels, the library said. "Finlay Donovan is Killing It," her first novel for adults, began a mystery series.</p><p>The fall book sale runs Friday, Nov. 6, through Sunday, Nov. 8, at Clara B. Mounce Public Library in historic downtown Bryan, with proceeds supporting the library system. Friends of the Library members may shop first on Friday evening and early Saturday. The sale opens to the general public from 9:30 a.m. to 2 p.m. Saturday and from 1:30 to 3:30 p.m. Sunday.</p><p>The library system is a partnership between Bryan and College Station that lets residents use one card at its three locations: Clara B. Mounce Public Library and the Carnegie History Center in Bryan and the Larry J. Ringer Library in College Station. In a recent episode of College Station''s city podcast, Ringer branch manager Derika Bailey described the system''s free programs for adults and teens, online tutoring and language-learning databases, meeting rooms and digital audiobooks.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Bestselling author Elle Cosimano speaks Nov. 4 at the Larry J. Ringer Library, and the Friends of the Library fall book sale runs Nov. 6-8 at Clara B. Mounce Public Library in downtown Bryan.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ln-10.webp', '2026-10-26T13:45:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Bryan + College Station Public Library System','libraries','books','Bryan','College Station']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Prices (per-bag book prices and the Sunday discount) were in the library''s post but left out per style rules.
• 확인할 점: Registration is required for the free book; registration link omitted (no URLs in body).
• 확인할 점: Members-only shopping hours: Friday 4-6 p.m. and Saturday 9-9:30 a.m. per library; summarized as ''Friday evening and early Saturday''.
• 확인할 점: ''Proceeds supporting the library system'' is based on the library''s description of the sale as a way to support the system.
• 확인할 점: Dateline College Station chosen because the author event is there; the book sale is in Bryan.
• 원문(Bryan + College Station Public Library System — 2026 Author Series): https://www.bcslibrary.org/programs/author-series/
• 원문(Bryan + College Station Public Library System — Friends of the Library Fall 2026 Book Sale is Nov. 6-8 (Oct. 8, 2026)): https://www.bcslibrary.org/friends-of-the-library-fall-2026-book-sale-is-nov-6-8/
• 원문(City of College Station — Podcast: More Than Books: Inside the Bryan + College Station Public Library System (Oct. 23, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/10/23/podcast-more-than-books-inside-the-bryan-college-station-public-library-system/
• 이용 조건: Text: facts from library system and City of College Station pages, rewritten / Photo: Unsplash License, Tom Hermans / Unsplash, https://unsplash.com/photos/book-lot-on-table-9BoqXzEeQqM
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('college-station', 'published', 'Lick Creek Park in the Dark Returns With Overnight Campouts Nov. 6 and Nov. 13', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Families can spend a night under the stars at Lick Creek Park next month as College Station Parks and Recreation brings back its Lick Creek Park in the Dark campouts.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-cv-05.webp" alt="A tent under a star-filled night sky (file photo). It was not taken at Lick Creek Park. Photo: Ahmet Yüksek / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ A tent under a star-filled night sky (file photo). It was not taken at Lick Creek Park. Photo: Ahmet Yüksek / Unsplash</em></p><p>The overnight events are scheduled for Friday, Nov. 6, and Friday, Nov. 13, at the Gary Halter Nature Center, the city said in a news release Monday. Each campout runs from 6 p.m. to 7 a.m. the next morning.</p><p>Check-in and campsite setup begin at 6 p.m., followed by a hot dog dinner at 7 p.m. and night hikes at 7:30 p.m., according to the schedule the city released. Presentations and hikes end at 9 p.m., and quiet hours begin at 10 p.m.</p><p>Weather permitting, campers can look through telescopes with the Brazos Valley Astronomy Club, explore nature at night with the Brazos Valley Chapter of the Texas Master Naturalist, see live invertebrates with the Texas A&amp;M Entomology Club and hike with the Rio Brazos Audubon Society, the city said.</p><p>Spots are limited, and registration closes the Wednesday before each campout. An adult 18 or older must accompany children, and pets and alcohol are not allowed, according to the city.</p><p>Campers must bring their own tents and supplies. The city recommends sleeping bags, flashlights, a tent lantern, bug spray and folding chairs, and noted that the site has no electricity.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'College Station Parks and Recreation will host overnight family campouts at Lick Creek Park''s Gary Halter Nature Center on Nov. 6 and Nov. 13, with night hikes and stargazing.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-cv-05.webp', '2026-10-26T14:10:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['College Station','Lick Creek Park','camping','parks and recreation','family events']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Per-person fee in the source omitted per style rules.
• 확인할 점: Outdoor activities are weather-dependent per the city.
• 원문(City of College Station — Pack your tents: Lick Creek Park in the Dark returns for November overnights (Oct. 26, 2026)): https://www.cstx.gov/news/posts/pack-your-tents-lick-creek-park-in-the-dark-returns-for-november-overnights/
• 원문(City of College Station blog — full post (Oct. 26, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/10/26/pack-your-tents-lick-creek-park-in-the-dark-returns-for-november-overnights/
• 이용 조건: Text: facts from City of College Station releases, rewritten / Photo: Unsplash License, Ahmet Yüksek / Unsplash, https://unsplash.com/photos/a-green-tent-sitting-under-a-night-sky-filled-with-stars-edw_zCMCrVo
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('business', 'published', 'College Station Alcohol Sales Rise 22% in September, State Data Show', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Bars, restaurants and other businesses with mixed beverage permits in College Station reported $8.48 million in alcohol sales in September, up nearly 22% from $6.96 million a year earlier, according to Texas Comptroller data.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-bz-10.webp" alt="Cocktails on a bar counter (file photo, 2023; not taken in College Station or Bryan). Photo: Daniel / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ Cocktails on a bar counter (file photo, 2023; not taken in College Station or Bryan). Photo: Daniel / Unsplash</em></p><p>The figures come from mixed beverage gross receipts that permit holders report to the state each month. In College Station, September receipts included $4.48 million from liquor, $3.33 million from beer and $653,000 from wine, the data show.</p><p>The largest single reporting location in College Station reported about $2.37 million in September, up from about $1.45 million a year earlier. Excluding that location, receipts at other College Station locations rose about 11%.</p><p>In Bryan, permit holders reported $1.76 million in September, up about 1% from a year earlier.</p><p>For the first nine months of the year, College Station locations reported $51.2 million in receipts, up 5.6% from the same period in 2025, while Bryan locations reported $16.9 million, up 3.7%, according to the state data.</p><p>The state collects a mixed beverage tax on these sales and sends part of it back to the cities and counties where the sales happen. Monthly figures can change as late reports and amendments come in.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Bars and restaurants with College Station addresses reported $8.48 million in alcohol sales in September, up nearly 22% from a year earlier, state data show. Bryan''s sales were nearly flat.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-bz-10.webp', '2026-10-26T15:20:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['College Station','Bryan','mixed beverage','restaurants','Texas Comptroller']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Data from Texas Open Data Portal dataset naix-2893 (Mixed Beverage Gross Receipts), updated Oct. 24, 2026; grouped by location_city in Brazos County (county code 21). Totals are our sums; September may still change with late filings (132 College Station locations reported for Sept. 2026 vs 133 in Sept. 2025).
• 확인할 점: The data do not show the cause of the increase; we did not attribute it to football. The single largest reporting location (a Texas A&M University concessions permit, not named in text) accounted for much of it.
• 확인할 점: We did not confirm the number of home football games in Sept. 2025 vs Sept. 2026; not stated in body.
• 확인할 점: Excludes a few tiny records filed under ''COLLEGE STA'' ($549 in Sept. 2025).
• 원문(Texas Comptroller of Public Accounts via Texas Open Data Portal — Mixed Beverage Gross Receipts (updated Oct. 24, 2026)): https://data.texas.gov/resource/naix-2893.json
• 이용 조건: Text: Texas state public data, rewritten / Photo: Unsplash License, Daniel / Unsplash, https://unsplash.com/photos/two-glasses-of-different-types-of-drinks-on-a-bar-wUZbQhehzJQ
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('opinion', 'draft', 'Our View: Whatever Bryan Decides on Medians, the Next Step Runs Through TxDOT', '<p>BRYAN — Few local issues have stirred Bryan residents like the raised medians on Texas Avenue. On Nov. 3, voters will decide Proposition A, a citizen-petitioned charter amendment that would require the medians to be removed from Rosemary Drive to about 600 feet south of State Highway 21. Whatever the outcome, this newspaper believes the city owes residents a clear, public road map for what comes next.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-09.webp" alt="An aerial view of a divided road with a landscaped median (file photo, 2021; taken in Petach Tikva, Israel, not Texas Avenue in Bryan). Photo: DoLiKs / Pexels"><p><em>▲ An aerial view of a divided road with a landscaped median (file photo, 2021; taken in Petach Tikva, Israel, not Texas Avenue in Bryan). Photo: DoLiKs / Pexels</em></p><p>The facts make that road map essential. Texas Avenue is a state road controlled by the Texas Department of Transportation, the city says. In an outside legal opinion that the City Council voted Oct. 13 to make public, the city''s attorneys concluded that Bryan would probably violate the Texas Transportation Code if it tried to remove or alter the medians without TxDOT''s written consent or an agreement giving the city control. The memo says a lawsuit or an opinion from the Texas attorney general may be needed if the amendment passes.</p><p>The price tag matters, too. The city estimates removal would cost $8 million and says it would have to find a funding source, which could squeeze other capital projects. If voters approve the amendment, the city says, the medians will not disappear overnight: legal review, talks with TxDOT, engineering, funding decisions and construction bids would all come first.</p><p>The council deserves credit for releasing a legal memo it could have kept confidential, and for showing that change is possible through TxDOT. In January, it backed removing a section of median and adding a traffic signal at the Brookside and Garden Acres intersection in coordination with the state, and city engineers say they are studying other locations. But residents on both sides of Proposition A should not have to guess at what happens next.</p><p>We urge the city to commit now, before the votes are counted, to publish a timeline within weeks of the election: when it will meet with TxDOT, what it will ask for, how it would pay for any changes and when the public will hear back. Early voting ends Friday. Whichever way Bryan votes, a clear plan is the best way to respect that choice.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Draft editorial: Bryan''s own legal opinion says the city likely cannot remove Texas Avenue medians without TxDOT. Voters deserve a clear, public plan for what happens after Nov. 3.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-09.webp', null::timestamptz, array['editorial','Texas Avenue','medians','Proposition A','Bryan']::text[], '• 확인할 점: AI DRAFT: This is an AI-written draft editorial. The editor must rewrite it in the paper''s own voice and decide the position before publishing. Do not publish as is.
• 확인할 점: The draft takes no side on Proposition A itself; it argues only for a public post-election plan. The editor should decide whether the paper endorses or opposes the measure.
• 확인할 점: Ballot language and segment from the City of Bryan ''Notice of Special Election'' (Oct. 12, 2026). Legal memo summary, $8 million estimate and next steps from the city''s Oct. 13, 2026 post. The memo''s author/law firm is not named on the city page (news outlets name Bojorquez; not used).
• 확인할 점: Brookside/Garden Acres decision ''in January'' (2026) and ongoing engineering evaluations from the city''s Aug. 11, 2026 post.
• 확인할 점: Advocates (BCS Medians Survey Group) were reported by other outlets as saying TxDOT expressed no opposition, and KBTX reported a crash analysis; neither was confirmed in an official source, so both are left out. TxDOT has not been contacted.
• 확인할 점: ''Early voting ends Friday'' assumes publication before Oct. 30. Update if published later.
• 확인할 점: Overlap: the news story ''Bryan Releases Legal Memo on Texas Avenue Medians Vote'' is already published; this is the editorial view on the same facts.
• 확인할 점: Photo: Brazos County Courthouse (Brazos County runs the election). Check that no other bcs2 story uses this file.
• 원문(City of Bryan — City of Bryan Sharing External Legal Opinion for Medians Charter Amendment (Oct. 13, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/city-of-bryan-sharing-external-legal-opinion-for-medians-charter-amendment/
• 원문(City of Bryan — Notice of Special Election (Oct. 12, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/notice-of-special-election/
• 원문(City of Bryan — City of Bryan Shares Information About Charter Amendments & Evaluates Potential Changes to Texas Avenue Medians (Aug. 11, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/city-of-bryan-shares-information-about-charter-amendments-evaluates-potential-changes-to-texas-avenue-medians/
• 이용 조건: Text: original opinion draft based on City of Bryan public documents / Photo: Pexels License, DoLiKs / Pexels, https://www.pexels.com/photo/aerial-photography-of-gray-roads-7018775/
• 상태: 의견 기사라 작성 중(초안). 편집장이 고쳐 쓴 뒤 발행'),
  ('sports', 'published', 'Aggie Volleyball Sweeps No. 4 Kentucky; Stowers, Sharp Earn SEC Honors', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Texas A&amp;M volleyball''s two road wins last weekend, capped by a sweep of No. 4 Kentucky, earned SEC weekly honors for Kyndal Stowers and Eliza Sharp and lifted the Aggies two spots in the national poll, the school said Monday.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-sp-09.webp" alt="A volleyball resting on a net post (file photo, 2025; not taken at Texas A&amp;M or Kentucky). Photo: Francesco Solomita / Unsplash"><p><em>▲ A volleyball resting on a net post (file photo, 2025; not taken at Texas A&amp;M or Kentucky). Photo: Francesco Solomita / Unsplash</em></p><p>The then-No. 19 Aggies beat Kentucky 25-20, 25-23, 25-21 on Sunday at Memorial Coliseum in Lexington, Kentucky, in a rematch of the 2025 NCAA championship match, according to Texas A&amp;M Athletics. The win snapped Kentucky''s 30-game home winning streak and was the first time the Wildcats had been swept at home since 2024. It was Texas A&amp;M''s second win this season over a top-five team.</p><p>Stowers had 16 kills and 10 digs against Kentucky, and Natalie Ring added 12 kills. Sharp had seven blocks, Taryn Morris matched her career high with five, and Margot Manning set a career high with 36 assists.</p><p>"It''s the way we played volleyball," head coach Jamie Morrison said after the match, according to remarks posted by the athletics department. "The way that we stayed composed, we stayed steady."</p><p>Two days earlier, Texas A&amp;M won 3-1 at Georgia (25-23, 18-25, 25-21, 25-23). Stowers had 14 kills and 16 digs there, and the Aggies served a season-high nine aces.</p><p>The SEC on Monday named Stowers, a senior, its Offensive Player of the Week for the second time this season and Sharp, a junior, its Defensive Player of the Week for the first time in her career. Sharp had 11 blocks over the weekend''s seven sets, according to Texas A&amp;M.</p><p>Texas A&amp;M (13-8, 6-3 SEC) is No. 17 in this week''s AVCA poll, up from No. 19, and has been ranked for 27 straight weeks. The Aggies return to Reed Arena to host No. 6 Tennessee at 6:30 p.m. Friday.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Texas A&M volleyball swept No. 4 Kentucky on the road, snapping the Wildcats'' 30-game home winning streak, and moved up to No. 17 before hosting No. 6 Tennessee at Reed Arena on Friday.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-sp-09.webp', '2026-10-26T17:20:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Texas A&M volleyball','Kentucky','Kyndal Stowers','Eliza Sharp','Reed Arena']::text[], '• 확인할 점: The release calls the match a ''National Title rematch'' but does not say who won the 2025 NCAA title; the story does not say either. Confirm on NCAA.com if the editor wants to add it.
• 확인할 점: Tennessee is No. 9 in the Sunday recap and No. 6 in Monday''s AVCA poll and honors release; No. 6 used.
• 확인할 점: Stowers'' consecutive double-double count differs between releases (fourth vs. fifth); not used.
• 확인할 점: Morrison''s quote is two sentences from a longer remark; the second is a sentence fragment as transcribed by the athletics department.
• 원문(Texas A&M Athletics — No. 19 Aggies Sweep No. 4 Kentucky in NCAA Title Rematch (Oct. 25, 2026)): https://12thman.com/news/2026/10/25/no-19-aggies-sweep-no-4-kentucky-in-ncaa-title-rematch
