  ('food-dining', 'published', 'Planning a Thanksgiving Turkey? CDC Says Start Thawing It Days Ahead', '<p>BRYAN — Thanksgiving falls on Nov. 26 this year, and federal food safety officials say the planning for a safe holiday meal starts well before the oven is turned on, especially for anyone cooking a frozen turkey.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-04.webp" alt="A raw whole turkey being prepared on a baking tray (file photo, 2020; posed stock image). Photo: RDNE Stock project / Pexels"><p><em>▲ A raw whole turkey being prepared on a baking tray (file photo, 2020; posed stock image). Photo: RDNE Stock project / Pexels</em></p><p>The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends thawing a turkey in the refrigerator and allowing about 24 hours for every 4 to 5 pounds. By that measure, a 20-pound bird needs four to five days in the refrigerator. Once thawed, it can stay there one to two days before cooking, the agency says.</p><p>For a faster option, the CDC says a turkey can be thawed in cold tap water in a leakproof bag, allowing about 30 minutes per pound and changing the water every 30 minutes. A microwave can also be used if the manufacturer''s directions allow it. In both cases, the bird should be cooked right after thawing. The agency warns never to thaw a turkey on the counter, because after two hours at room temperature it can enter the temperature range where germs grow, even if the center is still frozen.</p><p>Washing the raw bird is not recommended. Federal agencies have advised against rinsing turkey or chicken since 2005 because it can spread germs, the CDC says. It cites a 2020 survey in which 78% of U.S. adults said they wash or rinse turkey before cooking it.</p><p>The CDC recommends roasting in an oven set to at least 325 degrees and using a food thermometer to make sure the turkey reaches 165 degrees in the thickest part of the breast, the thigh joint and the wing joint, avoiding bone. It suggests cooking stuffing in a separate dish. Stuffing cooked inside the bird should also reach 165 degrees at its center.</p><p>Leftovers should go into the refrigerator within two hours of cooking, or within one hour if the food has been sitting somewhere warmer than 90 degrees, the agency says. Cutting large pieces into smaller portions helps them cool faster. Cooked turkey should be eaten within three to four days or frozen, and leftovers should be reheated to 165 degrees.</p><p>The stakes are real. The CDC says Clostridium perfringens, the second most common bacterial cause of food poisoning, causes outbreaks most often in November and December, frequently linked to holiday foods such as turkey and roast beef.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'A frozen turkey needs about 24 hours in the refrigerator for every 4 to 5 pounds, the CDC says, so a 20-pound bird needs four to five days. The agency also urges cooking it to 165 degrees.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-ls-04.webp', '2026-10-27T07:45:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Thanksgiving','food safety','turkey','CDC']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Main source is the CDC page ''Preparing Your Holiday Turkey Safely'' (dated April 29, 2024). The USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service pages and foodsafety.gov returned HTTP 403 to automated access, so USDA FSIS guidance (e.g., Meat and Poultry Hotline hours) was not used.
• 확인할 점: Thanksgiving 2026 date (Thursday, Nov. 26) is calendar math (fourth Thursday of November).
• 확인할 점: The ''20-pound bird needs four to five days'' line is arithmetic from CDC''s ''24 hours for every 4 to 5 pounds''.
• 확인할 점: Temperatures are Fahrenheit (written as ''degrees'' in AP style).
• 확인할 점: Photo: Commons lists it as both CC BY 2.0 (Flickr) and public domain as a USDA employee work; image is 800x600. Commons page does not describe people; check for faces.
• 원문(CDC — Preparing Your Holiday Turkey Safely (April 29, 2024)): https://www.cdc.gov/food-safety/foods/holiday-turkey.html
• 이용 조건: Text: original wording based on CDC guidance (U.S. federal work, public domain) / Photo: Pexels License, RDNE Stock project / Pexels, https://www.pexels.com/photo/person-holding-raw-turkey-on-a-baking-tray-5847620/
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('bryan', 'published', 'Bryan Trunk or Treat Collects Canned Food for Food Bank on Halloween Morning', '<p>BRYAN — Families can trade canned goods for a Halloween morning of candy and games Saturday, when the City of Bryan holds its ninth annual Trunk or Treat So Kids Can Eat at Bryan High School.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-cv-01.webp" alt="A car trunk decorated with a jack-o&#x27;-lantern theme at a trunk-or-treat event in Darien, Illinois (file photo, 2012; not from Bryan&#x27;s event). Photo: Jan Hardy / Wikimedia Commons (CC0)"><p><em>▲ A car trunk decorated with a jack-o''-lantern theme at a trunk-or-treat event in Darien, Illinois (file photo, 2012; not from Bryan''s event). Photo: Jan Hardy / Wikimedia Commons (CC0)</em></p><p>The event runs from 10 a.m. to 1 p.m. on Oct. 31 in the Bryan High School parking lot, according to the city''s Parks and Recreation Department. It benefits the Brazos Valley Food Bank, the regional nonprofit that distributes food to people in need.</p><p>Admission is five canned goods per person, the city said. Halloween costumes are encouraged.</p><p>Planned activities include trunk-or-treating at decorated vehicles, inflatables, a showing of the animated film "Hotel Transylvania," face painting, a photo booth, arts and crafts, and food vendors, according to the city''s event page.</p><p>The event is hosted by the city''s Parks and Recreation Department and Bryan High School. The deadline for organizations to sign up to decorate a trunk was Oct. 16.</p><p>The city''s online calendar also lists a separate Trunk or Treat at Legends Event Center, the city-owned sports and events venue in Travis Bryan Midtown Park, from 6 to 8:30 p.m. Wednesday, Oct. 28.</p><p>For trick-or-treating in neighborhoods on Halloween night, the Bryan Police Department recommends the hours of 6 to 8 p.m., the city said in a separate release Tuesday.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Bryan''s ninth Trunk or Treat So Kids Can Eat runs 10 a.m. to 1 p.m. Saturday at Bryan High School. Admission is five canned goods per person for the Brazos Valley Food Bank.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs2-cv-01.webp', '2026-10-27T08:20:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Bryan','Halloween','Trunk or Treat','Brazos Valley Food Bank','Parks and Recreation']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Event details are from the city''s undated Trunk or Treat event page (read Oct. 27) and the Aug. 24 fall programs release; confirm no weather change or relocation before Saturday.
• 확인할 점: The Legends Event Center trunk-or-treat appears only as a calendar listing (Oct. 28, 6-8:30 p.m.); the event detail page on events.bryantx.gov returned 403 (blocked), so no further details were used. Organizer not confirmed.
• 확인할 점: The 6-8 p.m. recommendation is from Bryan''s Oct. 27 ''Trick-or-Treating Safety Tips'' release, which the ln writer covered as its own story; kept to one line here.
• 확인할 점: Photo: description says the trunk has a jack-o''-lantern theme; editor should check the image for a visible license plate before publishing and crop if needed.
• 원문(City of Bryan — Trunk or Treat So Kids Can Eat event page (read Oct. 27, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/special-events/trunk-or-treat-so-kids-can-eat/
• 원문(City of Bryan — Parks, Recreation & Facility Services events list (read Oct. 27, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/parks-and-recreation/
• 원문(City of Bryan — City calendar listing (read Oct. 27, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/calendar/
• 원문(City of Bryan — Trick-or-Treating Safety Tips (Oct. 27, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/trick-or-treating-safety-tips/
• 이용 조건: Text: facts from City of Bryan public pages, rewritten / Photo: CC0 (VRT-confirmed), Jan Hardy, File:Halloween Trunk-or-Treat.jpg
